# My Songs: essential rules and task-start guide

Read this summary at task start and material handoffs. It routes to the full rules retained below and the named maintained workflows. Do not assume a long injected excerpt contains the complete instructions: search this file's headings and read the sections relevant to the actual task. A summary does not waive a narrower approval, rights, privacy or verification requirement. Apply Kieran's latest direct instructions to the authorised scope.

## Search narrowly and exclude generated storage

Kieran, 2 November 2026: do not repeatedly scan drives for routine context. Be intelligent about search scope and exclude huge build/cache subfolders. Apply across chats, agents and automations; this limits discovery cost without preventing useful searches of the owning project's source.

- Start with the supplied path, known task receipts, current project instructions, maintained tool registry and exact owning repository. Prefer direct reads and filename discovery within a named small subtree before searching file contents. Never start a routine lookup at a drive root, all of My Songs, all repositories/worktrees, or the whole Tools/Reports tree. Widen only to a specific likely location after a bounded miss; a genuinely broad inventory requires an explicit task need or Kieran's request.
- Exclude generated storage before recursion: dependency/package stores (`node_modules`, `.pnpm-store`, `.uv-cache`), virtual environments (`.venv`, `venv`), `.git` internals, build/output trees (`build`, `dist`, `target`, `obj` and actual generated `bin` directories), caches/test temp trees (`.cache`, `.tmp`, `.pytest_cache`, `.mypy_cache`, `.ruff_cache`, `__pycache__`), model weights/caches (`models`), generated media/render intermediates, and unrelated copied repositories/worktrees. Search such material only when that exact material is the subject, in its smallest relevant subtree. Never read a whole binary/media/model tree to locate a document or setting.
- Avoid blanket `--hidden`. An explicit positive `rg -g/--glob` can override ignore rules: put generated-storage exclusions after inclusion globs and verify the intended scope. Restrict content searches to relevant text/source types, sensible file sizes and a small thread count when storage is contended. `rg -m` limits matches per file, not total work; `Select-Object -First`, output/token limits and `yield_time_ms` do not impose a reliable scan deadline.
- Keep every launched search owned until it exits or is cancelled. Retain and inspect the full command result (`session_id`, `exit_code`, output), rather than printing only `r.output`. A completed orchestration cell is not proof that its child command completed. If a command yields, poll it or cancel the actual owned process/tree and verify exit before widening the search or handing off. Stopping an outer `functions.exec` cell alone is not process-cleanup evidence. No unattended/background discovery scans.
- Do not inspect `.codex-relocated-home`, sessions, browser profiles, caches or private application stores as generic context search roots. Exact saved command/session records may be read in bounded form for an explicitly requested forensic diagnosis; this exception does not permit recursive history scans or browser-session inspection.
- Reuse retained findings instead of rescanning. If the bounded route does not locate the answer, state the precise missing path or evidence and continue independent work. A saved policy is not proof that already-running chats have adopted it or that a runtime cancellation defect is fixed.

**Cross-chat blogworthiness reminder throttle — Kieran, 26 October 2026:** each chat remains responsible for recording its own completion assessment. A reminder controller may contact another chat only after that chat has had no activity for at least five minutes, and at most once during that continuous idle episode. Persist the thread ID, observed last-activity value and whether the episode was reminded or already satisfied. Immediately before sending, re-read the target and suppress the request if it is active, its activity changed, the five-minute quiet period has not elapsed, or its latest completed turn already records Blogworthiness: PASS or Blogworthiness: FAIL with a reason and evidence. Repeated polls stay silent. Re-arm only after genuinely newer activity; if the chat later becomes idle again, begin a new five-minute quiet period and allow at most one new reminder. Keep polling and reminder receipts private.

**Scheduled-job browser access — Kieran, 28 October 2026:** every scheduled job may use the ChatGPT desktop app's built-in in-app browser (`@Browser`) whenever it materially serves the job's existing authorised purpose. This supersedes the older in-app-browser disablement for scheduled runs only; it does not widen publication, send, account, spending, rights, privacy or other action authority. Prefer a sufficient supported connector/API/CLI when it is more reliable. Social marketing reporting jobs should use the in-app browser for remaining native dashboard checks after sufficient connector reads. Preserve private evidence and other owners' tabs or unsaved work, close completed job-created and released borrowed tabs, and verify a fresh tab inventory before handoff. If `@Browser` is unavailable in a scheduled run, record the precise capability hold rather than claiming access or silently switching to whole-PC control. This access change does not prove that the underlying lifecycle defect is fixed.

1. **Finish the authorised work.** Complete safe research, preparation, reversible fixes and verification before handoff. Preserve other owners' changes; use private `.codex/coordination/` records or participating-agent tools. Do not wake unrelated chats or send routine coordination into their conversations. Record precise blockers and continue unaffected work.
2. **Assess blogworthiness every time.** Before task or automation completion record PASS/FAIL, reason and evidence. PASS also records the reader-interest angle, duplicate review and next action/post URL or exact delivery blocker. Technical, creative, research and useful work-in-progress stories qualify without publicity or workflow improvement. Use `Workflows/blogworthiness-completion.md` and `skill-source/project-blog-writer/SKILL.md`; administration and posting-about-posting alone FAIL.
3. **Protect private material and commitments.** Keep credentials, correspondence, contacts, rights/permission evidence, private masters and deliberately unreleased material controlled. No new spending, paid routes, rights commitments, sensitive disclosure or account/access change without applicable authority. Preserve destination consent and exact-action gates. Never infer clearance from a public link.
4. **Keep evidence states exact.** Distinguish proposals, prepared files, drafts, uploads, sends, persisted schedules, publication, playback and verified outcomes. Unknown/unavailable is not zero. Read back material fields before commitment and independently verify persistence and the actual result. Stop after uncertain mutations; never blindly retry.
5. **Use the right browser/transport.** Scheduled jobs may use the in-app browser under the 28 October exception above; other chats and tasks remain under the 19 October stability disablement. Prefer supported connectors/APIs/CLIs for semantic operations when sufficient. Outside a scheduled run, use the external Chrome/Edge extension when interactive UI is required; an unavailable connection is a hold, not permission to use IAB. For signed-out previews/QA, use bounded isolated Playwright when it is the safer route, close pages/contexts/browser in `finally` and verify helper exit. Close finished job-created and released borrowed tabs; preserve others' active work. No profile/session-store inspection or browser/Codex termination as cleanup.
6. **Use and improve maintained tools.** Read `DanceFlow/ecosystem.md`, `Workflows/scheduled-danceflow-tool-use.md` and relevant Potential problems; run `Tools/DanceFlow.ps1 -Releases` when available and verify the runtime actually used. FrameRudiments owns offline video composition; StemLab owns audio/timing evidence; DanceRudiments owns native movement; DanceMoves owns live web motion. Compose supported contracts. Implement worthwhile reusable fixes in their canonical repositories and publish tested authorised software through established CI/GitHub/registry releases. Do not invent extra analysis or generation to exercise a tool. Short Shifter is independent of DanceFlow. Models/caches belong in the owning project's ignored `models/`.
7. **Choose render ownership from scope and measured cost.** Run a render through an operating-system-owned scheduler or service only when it is a full-length video or a representative preflight predicts more than 15 minutes. Keep every other render inside the active Codex session; high-quality settings or the 30-second chunk boundary alone do not make it a background job. Record the estimate and decision before production encoding, and never migrate or duplicate a live render. Qualified background jobs still require verified ancestry, persisted identity/state/log paths and ordinary storage, chunk/resume, decode/hash and attended-review safeguards. FrameRudiments 0.4.14+ `render-progress.json` and stderr heartbeats are status evidence, not completion evidence.
8. **Preserve EPK authority and appearance.** Authenticated EPK reads/writes use the documented WordPress REST API and encrypted Application Password: `Workflows/wordpress-epk-rest-api.md`. Merge fresh `content.raw`, hash-gate the reviewed base/candidate, send only approved fields and require exact readback. WordPress-selected lyric/cue attachments are the sole timing-file authority; no page-local timing URLs/fallbacks. Preserve canonical audio/master associations, lyrics, cues, media, controls, accessibility, approved choreography and mobile-motion bindings. Verify anonymous desktop/tablet/mobile output and applicable timing/playback/input routes; synthetic evidence is not physical-phone or attended-listening evidence. Public saves retain their exact gate except eligible verified publicity-only additions under the limited standing permission.
9. **Publish and reconcile accurately.** Social-media post/publish requests mean future scheduling unless Kieran explicitly requests immediate publication for the item. Website updates and other non-social publications may proceed when authorised; preserve any explicitly requested future date. Begin applicable YouTube/TikTok routes first; explicit destination-only requests control scope. Verify the persisted date/time/timezone and later public output. Same-job reconcile verified campaign media and publicity into release records and relevant EPKs using `Workflows/campaign-media-epk-sync.md` and `Workflows/verified-song-publicity.md`; preserve original-source attribution and public-use eligibility. Do not republish passing pages or expose private/scheduled material.
10. **Guard every external reply/submission.** Read `Workflows/outreach-email-reply-audit.md` and `Tools/Outreach Send Gate/README.md`, inspect private shared records, atomically claim the canonical venue and exact original obligation, then independently recheck current Sent/submission evidence immediately before sending. No owned claim, conflict or uncertain prior send means hold. Preserve exact-payload approval; different wording or a split thread does not make a new obligation.
11. **Make media and copy deliberate.** Include useful actual screenshots/videos and clearly labelled generated illustrations in documentation. Blogs need fresh article-specific pictorial featured artwork plus relevant in-body media; dynamic subjects need an actual demonstration. Campaign fonts must be intentionally selected, licence-cleared, saved and pinned. Preserve canonical lyrics; clean edits need their supported workflow. Use `skill-source/speak-like-kieran/SKILL.md`, current campaign keyword policy/register and maintained visual specifications for public copy. Verify delivered-size readability, safe zones, responsive layout and playback as applicable.
12. **Retain coherent local records.** Use one Europe/London-date report per campaign run, inspect same-date records before acting, and preserve older history. Reconcile canonical plans, ledgers, mirrors and generator inputs with verified changes. Source evidence and exact runtime identities outrank filenames or old notes. Inspect temporary-directory access before substantial runs. Never close/restart Codex or repair its live state without fresh explicit authority.

## Evidence-led hashtag discovery and use

For keyword/hashtag research, campaign copy, social preparation or relevant incidental evidence, follow `Workflows/hashtag-discovery-and-use.md` and `Workflows/keywordmoves-evidence-research.md`. Use KeywordMoves >=0.6.0 `hashtag-planner` with the existing source-native adapters: literal discovery, explicit exact-asset/platform review and final-copy audit. Preserve natural search phrases, hashtags, native topics/tags and video tags as different fields. Keep direct relevance, native safety/context, dates, geography, window and units; reject unrelated trends and generic reach bait, never pad a set or transfer popularity across platforms. Counts are conservative editorial ceilings, not platform limits or proven optima; Threads, Pinterest, community posts, websites and music metadata need their own native treatment. Check the guide before use, not an old copied tag block.

Merge every plausible new song/tag/platform proposal and changed evidence into the existing 21-column BOM keyword register through its guarded validator, preserving history and `proposed` state. Retain exact set/copy/asset hashes, native acceptance/schedule readback and like-for-like outcome reviews; whole-post success is not per-tag attribution. Refresh expired evidence and inspect `metadata.passed` from the copy audit. This adds no research quota, spend, account action, send, public save, post, schedule change or new notification; preserve current ownership, active-date/no-op/stop/batch rules and exact approvals. During a quiet/read-only job maintain only evidence actually encountered. Record the mandatory blogworthiness assessment in the existing report; configuration does not prove adoption or reach improvement.

## Read the detailed rules for the selected task

| Task | Required next reading |
| --- | --- |
| Any substantial task or scheduled run | Relevant full sections below; current private ownership records; ecosystem and scheduled-tool-use workflow; blogworthiness completion workflow |
| Blog article | Project blog documentation/publication; blog artwork; project-blog-writer and voice skills; `Workflows/blog-post-source-verification.md` |
| EPK/page update | WordPress EPK publisher; REST API, timing authority, canonical metadata, mobile motion, merge/preservation, Unicode and executable-code transport sections; corresponding workflows |
| Audio/video/Short/Canvas | StemLab, loop-selection and FrameRudiments skills; canonical master/lyrics/timing; campaign fonts, covers, first frames, 60 fps default, safe zones and relevant rendering workflows |
| Campaign/outreach/publicity | Release-local plan and visual/keyword policy; venue memory; duplicate-send gate; verified-publicity/media reconciliation; scheduling and platform-specific skills |
| Tool development/release | Owning repository AGENTS.md; current releases and capability docs; GitHub publication/release, public README and project-local-model rules |
| Browser or app problem | Browser disablement, ownership cleanup, field verification, lifecycle safety and relevant Potential problems; supported connection discovery only |

Full instructions and historical problem evidence follow. Keep this opening guide concise when adding future rules, update its routing when a critical rule changes, and preserve detailed source/approval requirements below. This is an instruction summary, not evidence that other running chats have read it.

## Mandatory blogworthiness check for every task and automation

Kieran, 21 October 2026: explicitly assess blogworthiness before completing every task, chat activity, agent assignment and automation run, including work outside My Songs. Keep this requirement near the start of instruction files and in every saved automation prompt so it does not depend on a later, truncated instruction block.

**Cross-chat reminder throttle — Kieran, 26 October 2026:** the completion requirement is primarily self-applied by the chat doing the work. Do not repeatedly message another chat merely because it remains idle or because a monitor runs again. A reminder controller may act only after the target has had no activity for at least five minutes, and only once during that continuous idle episode. Persist a private per-chat record containing the thread ID, observed last-activity value, idle-episode identity and whether the reminder was sent or the episode was already satisfied. Immediately before any reminder, re-read the target: suppress the send if it is active, its activity value changed, it has been idle for less than five minutes, or its latest completed turn already contains the required PASS/FAIL assessment. After a reminder or a satisfied episode, all later checks stay silent until the controller observes genuinely newer activity; that activity re-arms the chat, and a later five-minute idle period may receive one new reminder. Monitoring configuration, polling and ledger maintenance are private and must not themselves create user-visible status messages.

- Record `Blogworthiness: PASS` or `Blogworthiness: FAIL`, a short evidence-based reason and the source evidence in the existing task report or final handoff. Small tasks may use one sentence. A missing assessment is an incomplete handoff; a saved rule or prompt does not prove a running task applied it.
- PASS means enough verified, public-safe substance for a coherent story with a specific reader-interest angle: creative or musical progress; a useful technical feature, experiment, comparison, discovery, fix, failure or recovery; illuminating work in progress; a campaign milestone or other meaningful public development. Workflow improvement and publicity are possible angles, not prerequisites. Check current posts and pending drafts to prevent duplicate coverage.
- FAIL means trivial administration, substantial duplication, insufficient verified substance, no reader-interest angle, or privacy constraints that remove the point of the story. Do not create a post solely about applying this check, changing blogging rules or completing publication administration.
- Keep editorial merit separate from delivery. Missing artwork, access or a publication route is a named blocker on a passing story, not a reason to relabel it FAIL. For every PASS, record the angle, evidence, duplicate check and next action: covered by a linked existing post, prepared/draft, scheduled, published, verified live, or blocked with the smallest specific requirement and a named continuation. Do not let bundling postpone a ready story indefinitely.
- Use the maintained project-blog-writer skill and Kieran's voice guidance for qualifying articles. Preserve fresh pictorial featured artwork, relevant in-body rich media, actual demonstrations of dynamic features, source attribution, privacy, rights, publication timing and independent public verification. Never infer permission to disclose private records or publish unsafe material from a passing assessment.
- Add this explicit completion requirement when creating or updating an automation, but do not turn it into a user-visible nag. Start every saved prompt with the automation's real task and purpose, then place one compact quiet-record sentence immediately after that opening paragraph. Never prepend the full rubric, duplicate the requirement, create a standalone completion-check run, or notify merely to request the assessment. Preserve the automation's original purpose, schedule, paused/active state, destination owner, notification policy, stop rules, costs and other approvals. A quiet/no-op or read-only job still assesses its actual result and records it through its permitted report or handoff without inventing work or notifications.

For the full rubric, examples and machine-checkable retained assessments, see `Workflows/blogworthiness-completion.md` in My Songs or this package's `workflows/blogworthiness-completion.md`.

# My Songs project instructions

## Cross-chat duplicate-send gate

Kieran, 18 October 2026: before every external reply or submission, all chats must follow Workflows/outreach-email-reply-audit.md and Tools/Outreach Send Gate/README.md. Read shared private send-coordination records, claim the canonical venue and exact source obligation atomically, then independently recheck current Sent/submission evidence immediately before sending. The shared gate lives under Outreach Response Reports/send-coordination/. A different payload or split thread is not a different unanswered obligation. Do not send without a successful owned claim; a conflict, inaccessible claim store or uncertain prior send is a hold. Preserve exact-payload approval. Keep coordination private; do not wake unrelated chats or insert status prompts into them. Saved instructions are not evidence that another running chat has adopted the gate.

## Visual media in all documentation

Standing instruction from Kieran, 16 October 2026: all documentation must include screenshots, videos and generated images wherever possible. Apply across every project, chat, agent, workflow and recurring job, including READMEs, user and developer guides, tutorials, reports, and maintained workflow and skill documentation.

- Whenever creating or updating documentation, actively include relevant screenshots, demonstration videos and generated images that explain the subject, steps, interface or result. Use each medium wherever feasible and useful, and improve existing documentation when it is next touched.
- Capture screenshots and videos from the actual relevant interface, output or behaviour; identify the version or context when needed. Clearly label generated images as illustrations, concepts or mockups. Keep illustrations distinct from evidence of implemented features, completed actions or verified outcomes.
- Reuse suitable authorised media and the maintained capture, rendering and image-generation tools. Create new media when it adds explanatory value within the task's scope and existing cost limits.
- Place media beside the relevant explanation with clear captions, useful image alt text and video captions or transcripts where applicable. Use supported embeds or durable accessible links, retain user-controlled playback, and verify that images display, videos play and references resolve in the intended documentation format.
- Preserve privacy, rights and publication requirements; remove secrets and private information before inclusion. When a medium is unavailable, unsupported or adds no useful explanation, include the applicable media and record a concise reason for the omission in the existing job record. Do not claim that media was captured, generated or verified unless it was.

## Choose foreground or operating-system render ownership

Standing instruction from Kieran, 27 October 2026, superseding the broader 23 October rule: use an operating-system-owned scheduler or service only for a full-length video or when a bounded representative preflight predicts more than 15 minutes. Keep all other renders in the active Codex session. High-quality settings, a duration above FrameRudiments' 30-second chunk threshold, or scheduler availability alone do not qualify a render for background ownership.

- Before production encoding, estimate wall time from representative sequential composition evidence plus encoding, assembly and required QA overhead. Record the inputs, arithmetic, estimate, 15-minute comparison and foreground/background decision. If there is not enough evidence, run a bounded calibration inside Codex first rather than assuming a background job.
- Make the ownership decision once at the launch boundary. Do not migrate, restart or duplicate a live render because a later ETA changes. If a foreground attempt reaches a safe failure/retry boundary and its observed cost now predicts more than 15 minutes, a subsequent attempt may use the scheduler with that evidence.
- FrameRudiments projects longer than 30 seconds still use cue-aware sequential chunks by default; chunking is a recovery contract, not a background-ownership rule. Read `render-progress.json` or the flushed stderr heartbeat for regular state. Progress and ETA do not prove successful completion.
- **My Songs cache deployment rule — 27 October 2026:** every new FrameRudiments production render uses `C:\Users\Kieran\AppData\Local\FrameRudiments\cache` as its `--work-cache`/`FRAMERUDIMENTS_WORK_CACHE` root, keeping high-I/O work off the main `Z:` workspace until verified final promotion. Supply a complete `--reserve-gib` estimate so FrameRudiments physically reserves payload plus 2% at both cache and final locations. Run `framerudiments cache-status --work-cache "C:\Users\Kieran\AppData\Local\FrameRudiments\cache" --expect-empty` before an unrelated render, after every successful handoff, and at least once per active day while render work is underway. A known live job may make it nonempty; any nonempty cache without a verified live owner is a hold for evidence/resume inspection, never permission for blind deletion.

- For a qualifying background job on Windows, use Task Scheduler or another verified service-owned execution route. Record the task identity, launch time, run directory, worker PID, state/log paths and exact frozen inputs. Verify the live worker's ancestry or equivalent ownership evidence before describing it as independent.
- `Start-Process`, `-WindowStyle Hidden`, a new shell, background syntax and a `detached` label are not evidence of independence. A process that remains in Codex's Windows job fails this rule even when it has a different PID or no visible window.
- Keep bounded progress evidence and preserve interrupted attempts without overwriting them. When supported by the renderer, use finalized segments or resumable checkpoints; otherwise state that only monitoring evidence is recoverable and that an interrupted encode must restart.
- For every FrameRudiments video longer than 30 seconds, retain its default sequential chunk mode unless an exact compatibility need is recorded. Prefer boundaries at pinned verse, chorus, bridge, interlude or other structural cues. Never render feedback or temporal-effect ranges independently or in parallel: resume must verify the finalized contiguous prefix and replay it from frame zero to restore state before encoding the missing suffix. Stream-copy the verified video chunks, mux the canonical audio once, and require exact frame coverage, CFR timestamps, full decode, decoded hashes on both sides of every seam and a final file hash. Independently encoded H.264 chunks need gap-free decoded continuity; byte identity with a monolithic encode is not a claim.
- Before launch, calculate the complete peak requirement from named evidence and physically reserve all retained intermediate outputs, the final assembled deliverable and a 2% margin. Release only the matching stage reservation immediately before that stage writes its replacement bytes, and keep the margin reserved through final technical QA. Refuse startup when the complete reservation cannot be allocated. A sparse placeholder or earlier free-space reading does not reserve capacity. Use `Tools/Render Space/Reserve-RenderSpace.ps1` for My Songs render jobs and retain its plan, receipt, allocation delta and release events.
- Monitor the scheduler-owned task and render outputs independently. Completion still requires the expected dimensions/frame rate, finalized output, full decode, matching recorded hash and separate attended visual/listening approval.
- Before a substantial run, perform the required project-local temporary-directory create/read/atomic-rename/delete probe using the intended runtime and output parent. Record exact scheduler registration and startup failures rather than silently falling back to a Codex-owned process.

## Disable the in-app browser outside scheduled jobs while the IAB lifecycle defect remains open

Standing instruction from Kieran, 19 October 2026: do not open, select, control or reuse Codex's in-app browser while openai/codex issue #55159 remains unresolved, or until a verified installed release has fixed the stale-frame retry loop. Under Kieran's later direct instruction of 28 October 2026, this disablement no longer applies to scheduled jobs: every scheduled run may invoke the built-in in-app browser as `@Browser` when it materially serves the job's existing authorised purpose. The exception grants browser access only, preserves every narrower action gate, and is not evidence of a product repair. Non-scheduled My Songs chats and agents remain under the disablement unless Kieran directly re-enables the browser for their exact task.

- Use a purpose-built connector, supported API or CLI for the semantic operation whenever one is available. A linked page or earlier browser use does not make the rest of the workflow browser-first.
- Outside a scheduled run, when interactive browser UI is genuinely required, explicitly select the user's external Chrome or Edge through the ChatGPT browser extension. Keep that browser family fixed for the task. If the extension connection is unavailable, record the hold and direct Kieran to **Settings → Computer use** to install or reconnect it; do not silently fall back to the in-app browser.
- For signed-out public-page checks, local previews, screenshots and responsive QA that do not need the user's browser session, prefer a bounded standalone Playwright/Chromium run in the owning project. Use an isolated job-local profile, do not copy cookies or inspect browser profiles, close every created page/context/browser in `finally`, and verify that the helper process exits.
- Outside a scheduled run, never fall back to the in-app browser when a connector, API, CLI, external-browser extension or bounded signed-out route is unavailable. Retain the prepared action and record the exact hold. A scheduled run may use `@Browser` under the 28 October exception; any other override still requires a later direct instruction from Kieran that explicitly re-enables the in-app browser for the exact task.
- Stop new IAB work immediately when any five-minute interval reaches 100 IAB lifecycle or missing-frame events, when missing-frame errors continue after navigation or teardown, or when an idle, failed or interrupted chat retains browser ownership. Preserve checkpoints and active uploads or unsaved forms, close verified completed tabs, and leave a precise hold for anything that cannot be released safely.
- Existing active non-scheduled runs do not prove adoption of this rule. At the next safe message boundary they must checkpoint, release completed IAB work and use the external or non-browser route on resumption. Scheduled jobs instead follow the 28 October access exception and must still apply the lifecycle stop condition above.

## Use intentionally selected campaign fonts

Standing instruction from Kieran, 19 October 2026: every campaign must use intentionally selected, licence-cleared typography that fits the song's visual language. Never use a default or generic system font such as Arial, Calibri, Times New Roman, Segoe UI or an unreviewed `sans-serif`/`serif` fallback for campaign artwork, lyric videos, visualisers, social assets, thumbnails, EPK display treatments or other designed campaign media unless Kieran explicitly requests that exact font.

- Choose the font from a reputable type library or the campaign's already approved assets. Prefer Google Fonts or another source with clear redistribution and embedding terms when a new open font is needed. Verify the current primary source and licence before use; do not rely on a font aggregator's claim alone.
- Select from the song's maintained visual specification and the actual reading context. Check expressive fit, lyric readability, weight, width, punctuation, diacritics and needed scripts. Cultural relevance must come from documented design context rather than stereotyped or novelty lettering.
- Save the exact approved font bytes and licence in the release-local campaign assets, record source URL, family, style or axes, retrieval date and SHA-256, and pin the file in deterministic render projects. Do not depend on a machine-installed copy or fetch a web font during rendering.
- Render representative long and short lyric lines at the delivery size and inspect the actual output. Keep an accessible fallback only for a verified missing-glyph or unavailable-font failure; treat fallback use as a visible hold, never as silent substitution.
- Preserve an approved campaign type system across later assets. Changing fonts is an art-direction change and needs evidence or Kieran's direction, rather than novelty for its own sake.

## Schedule social-media publication; honour explicit dates elsewhere

Clarification from Kieran, 23 October 2026: only social-media publication must be scheduled by default. This supersedes the broader 13 October wording that applied future scheduling to every publishing destination. Apply across My Songs chats, agents, campaigns and recurring workflows.

Standing exact-action authorisation from Kieran, 31 October 2026: when Kieran says **"post it", "publish it" or "schedule it"**, that instruction is the project-wide confirmation for the named, unchanged video or social item and its stated or contextually established destinations. Do not ask for another My Songs confirmation for that same action. Reconfirm only when the media, copy, destination, timing intent or other material payload changes; when a new rights, privacy, spend or account commitment appears; or when the destination itself requires an unavoidable security or terms handoff. A platform warning or failed/pending save still requires truthful readback, not another general permission question.

- For social posts and videos, use the social-video-scheduling skill and applicable release-video-social-scheduling workflow. Select an evidence-backed future slot unless Kieran explicitly requests immediate social publication. Verify the persisted date, time and timezone, then the later public result.
- Approved website maintenance, WordPress page corrections, blog publication and other non-social publishing do not require an artificial future slot. Complete the authorised action when ready, preserving the relevant exact-content, privacy, rights, deployment and verification gates.
- An explicitly approved future date remains authoritative for that item. This clarification does not move an already dated article or social post, change another job's schedule or authorise a public save whose content has not been approved.
- Retain prepared, saved, scheduled, published and independently verified states separately. A persisted automation definition proves configuration, not that its wakeup or publication actually ran.

## Publish to YouTube and TikTok first

Standing instruction from Kieran, 12 October 2026: whenever Kieran says to publish something, always start with YouTube and TikTok first, when applicable, before other publishing destinations. Apply to every project chat, agent, campaign, scheduled job and automation.

- Begin the applicable YouTube (including Shorts where appropriate) and TikTok upload, scheduling or publication routes before moving to other platforms. Choose actual posting times separately under the existing evidence-backed scheduling rule; priority does not require simultaneous or immediate publication.
- Judge applicability from the content, supported format and requested destination scope. Honour explicit destination-only requests and exclusions. If either platform is inapplicable or a verified blocker prevents its next authorised action, record the precise reason and continue the remaining authorised work.
- Preserve existing content, privacy, rights, approval and verification requirements. Record uploads, drafts, persisted schedules and verified publication distinctly, and complete the required same-job campaign-media EPK reconciliation after verified publication.

## Keep published campaign media in its EPK

Standing instruction from Kieran, 12 October 2026: whenever a project chat, agent, campaign or scheduled workflow publishes a campaign video, YouTube Short or social post, reconcile the same material into every relevant campaign EPK during that same job. Apply to past and future releases, recovering earlier omissions in campaign reviews and catalogue audits. Follow Workflows/campaign-media-epk-sync.md.

- Embed each distinct published YouTube video and Short wherever YouTube permits. For the same video published across several platforms, show one EPK item using its YouTube embed. Retain cross-platform receipts privately. Group only proven identical assets using exact render hashes or verified cross-post mappings; do not merge distinct edits, Shorts, performances or versions by similar titles.
- Verify exact campaign association, original publication and public availability. Keep publication, playback, embedding, persisted scheduling, drafts and local renders distinct. Reconcile scheduled items after verified publication; never expose embargoed, private or unlisted review material.
- Preserve the approved canonical full video in its existing hero. Add genuinely different Shorts and other posts cumulatively in a release-specific, accessible campaign-media section. Distinct text, image and carousel posts may use permissible same-site material, supported embeds or modest dated descriptions with direct original links. Artist promotion is not independent coverage or endorsement.
- Use titled, responsive YouTube iframes without autoplay and with a suitable portrait layout for Shorts. Preserve controls, fullscreen, keyboard access and privacy/consent behaviour. When YouTube embedding is unavailable, use the verified original campaign render: upload or reuse its exact bytes in WordPress and show one native, user-controlled HTML video player with its approved poster. Verify local/hosted hashes, attachment identity and actual playback. Keep YouTube restrictions unchanged; never obtain a file by bypassing access controls. Do not substitute another edit or publish an uncleared/private source. If the original or hosting is unavailable, retain a direct link and precise hold. A loaded iframe or upload receipt does not prove playback.
- Standing instruction from Kieran, 15 October 2026: omit redundant "Watch on YouTube" links beneath embedded videos and Shorts. Preserve each EPK's existing styled YouTube buttons, meaningful captions, source citations and link-only fallbacks for unavailable embeds. Remove only the matching embedded player's redundant watch anchor; keep all player, artwork, effect and timing source unchanged. Use Tools/EPK Campaign Media/clean_epk_video_links.py for exact-span preparation and retain its evidence.
- Integrate campaign media into each release’s design and narrative. Record page-owned tokens and reference sections, place the section before footer/contact/end matter, give full films cinematic width and compose Shorts as a portrait gallery using the existing typography, palette, border, depth and spacing language. A generic shared block appended at the end is a hold. Use the reviewed explicit profile with Tools/EPK Campaign Media/integrate_epk_media.py; the tool does not choose art direction or publish. Capture and compare actual desktop, tablet and mobile renders.
- Merge from fresh saved editor content, preserving history, media, design, controls, lyrics, downloads, timing metadata, choreography and mobile sensor bindings. Validate exact candidate hashes, Unicode, responsive rendering, playback and retained features. Re-read before saving to detect another owner's changes. Keep the existing exact-payload action-time public-save gate; only independently eligible publicity-only additions may use the limited standing publicity permission. This rule does not grant blanket public saves, uploads, redesigns, rights commitments or plugin deployments.
- Keep release-local campaign-media evidence with asset/group identity, platform IDs/URLs, publication and verification dates, public-use eligibility, EPK targets, candidate/saved hashes and precise recorded locally, staged, awaiting approval, published and verified-live states. Reconcile mirrors/generator inputs after verified publication. Passing EPKs need no repeat save. Complete unaffected work and keep necessary EPK actions outstanding until approval or a verified blocker is resolved.

The hero video must contain the exact release master soundtrack. Connect every verified full-master player to the same DanceMoves playback source so the existing effects, lyrics and cues follow the active player. Map verified partial-track Shorts with explicit song offset, soundtrack duration and any player preroll; verify the original upload file and render/trim evidence. Never infer a mapping from a title or duration alone. Preserve alternate-track associations and report missing provenance as a precise hold. Use the shared registerYouTube/registerPlayer APIs and metadata-derived timing sources; page callbacks describe only the existing appearance.

## Connect suitable EPK effects to mobile motion

- Standing instruction from Kieran, 11 October 2026: every EPK creation, edit, publication, shared motion upgrade, campaign review and recurring release/EPK audit must inventory pointer, hover, tilt, parallax, depth, lighting and directional effects and verify that effects suitable for phone movement have a working mobile sensor connection. Apply to past and future releases and every project chat, agent and scheduled workflow. Follow `Workflows/epk-mobile-motion.md`.
- Identify each effect's actual visual consumer, variables, bounds, acquisition owner, controller and neutral state. Check artwork, hero, below-fold cards and playback stages. A loaded plugin, listener, permission button, CSS variable or desktop mouse response alone does not prove mobile attachment. Keep static pages, ordinary button hover/focus feedback and scroll/navigation controls intentional; do not add movement solely to satisfy a checklist.
- Use the current published DanceMoves sensor/controller APIs. Bind mobile input to the same approved appearance mapping as pointer input; keep page-owned callbacks narrow. Share sensor acquisition and permission UI across related consumers. Extend an existing working route through supported subscription/composition rather than adding a competing listener, scheduler or page-specific shared-library branch. Preserve desktop pointer and keyboard/focus behaviour independently.
- Require a secure context and supported mobile/touch device. Request sensor permission only when required and only from an explicit visitor gesture; on other browsers use the supported direct-listener route. Distinguish eligible, permission requested/granted/denied, listening, finite data received and visibly active. Leave an accurate unavailable/blocked state when readings do not arrive. Never imply sensor delivery from a stored choice or permission response.
- Keep explicit page motion-off, reduced motion, forced colours, visibility, screen rotation, pagehide/pageshow, root removal and teardown authoritative. Reset pending input and visual targets appropriately; do not silently persist an opt-out when merely suspending a page or overwrite a visitor's choice on teardown. Keep input bounded, account for screen orientation and suppress stationary jitter. Preserve supported calibration/window settings unless a reviewed change is required.
- Verify fresh saved source and rendered desktop/tablet/mobile output, the full route from sensor event through controller to each required painted consumer, all motion switches, permission/denial/no-data paths, lifecycle resets and listener cleanup. Test input storms, meaningful bounds and any applicable adaptive downgrade/recovery. Label emulated or synthetic evidence accurately; physical phone/Safari behaviour and frame-rate acceptance need physical evidence. Record no unnecessary sensor route for intentionally static effects.
- Stage fixes from fresh editor content and current exact runtime/package identity. Preserve timing metadata, lyrics, media, controls, links, publicity, accessibility and approved choreography. Metadata-only input repairs do not authorise new rhythmic choreography. Keep staged, awaiting_publication_approval, published and verified_live states separate; obtain the existing exact-payload/action-time confirmation before public saves or plugin installation. Reconcile release-local mirrors and generator inputs after publication, and do not republish passing pages.
- Store project sources, candidates, preview assets, screenshots, evidence, reports, build caches and temporary outputs under `Z:\My Songs`. Publish reusable tool/workflow improvements to their established GitHub repositories and matching releases during the same job, preserving the separate public-deployment gate.

## Synchronize canonical song metadata into WordPress

Standing instruction from Kieran, 13 October 2026: whenever verified canonical BPM, lyric timing, cue timing or other critical song metadata changes, update every affected WordPress EPK during the same job. Apply to every project chat, release workflow and recurring audit. Saving a local canonical record alone does not complete the change.

- Resolve exact release, track and master identity from artist-approved authority and verified distributor records. Critical metadata includes BPM, canonical lyric wording and timing, cue timing, title/artist credits, official cover and public audio identity, release date/state, UPC/ISRC and verified platform links. Keep private masters, personal data, rights and permission evidence private. Do not infer a new authority from a filename, estimate or fallback.
- Capture fresh authenticated editor fields, selected attachment identities and canonical live presentation. Stage the smallest correction: BPM/metadata fields only where sufficient, exact canonical timing uploads/selections when required, or a narrowly merged visible-lyrics/fact/link change. Reconcile My Songs, calendars and social metadata when the changed fact affects them. Preserve all unrelated design, media, controls, mobile input, choreography and track associations.
- WordPress EPK Timing metadata remains the only timing-file authority. Players, renderers, cues and downloads consume its plugin-generated configuration; never insert timing URLs or a page-local source override. Validate UTF-8, exact hosted byte size/SHA-256, parser/event bounds and recording identity before selecting a file. Retain original attachments and backups; do not delete media or retime the recording to fit a BPM change.
- Complete the authorized WordPress synchronization, not merely an audit or local update. Honour mandatory tool/action-time approval gates; when one genuinely applies, present one exact narrow correction batch and finish unaffected work. Reuse already granted approval for unchanged exact targets/values/payloads; do not invent another general confirmation. Record a verified access, ownership, missing-source or approval blocker precisely and retain the correction for resumption.
- After saving, reload the editor and independently verify persisted values, anonymous public configuration, every dependent consumer, exact selected hosted bytes, parser state, retained features and representative responsive layouts. Keep property persistence, runtime loading, structural checks, synthetic motion and physical-phone evidence distinct. Restore temporary QA settings.
- Reconcile canonical records, fact sheets, asset manifests, attachment maps, current-source-authority, local mirrors and generator inputs with the verified WordPress result; update the existing dated reports and current audit states. A previously unknown BPM that an artist confirms becomes a known correction, not an unresolved source hold. Passing pages need no repeat save.

## WordPress metadata is the only EPK timing-file authority

- Standing instruction from Kieran, 11 October 2026: every EPK player, lyric renderer, cue consumer and timing-file download must reference the timing attachments selected in that page's WordPress EPK Timing metadata. Never hardcode lyric or cue timing URLs in an EPK's content, scripts, data attributes, template, generated payload or fallback. Concrete URLs belong only in the canonical metadata and its plugin-generated runtime configuration.
- Use the supported metadata-derived runtime contract (`danceMovesConfig.lyricTimingUrl` and `danceMovesConfig.cueTimingUrl` in the current WordPress integration). Keep exact URLs, query parameters and percent encoding intact. Resolve the configuration before starting a consumer; changing the selected attachment must update every dependent consumer after reload without a body edit. Do not create an independent page-local timing-source override.
- Bind timing-download controls to the same metadata-derived source. When a required source is unavailable, retain the audio and readable lyrics, expose an accurate unavailable state and disable the corresponding download; never guess, silently fall back to an old hardcoded URL or clear working metadata automatically.
- Multi-track or alternate-version pages must preserve the supported exact track/master association; a page-level field is not authority to apply one arbitrary timing file to distinct tracks. Preserve approved clean-version transformations and cue choreography. Missing mappings or approval remain explicit holds.
- On every EPK publication and weekly run, scan fresh saved content and rendered HTML for hardcoded timing-file literals, compare all consumers/downloads with persisted selected attachments, verify hosted bytes/parser state, and prove a metadata-only source change reaches the consumers. Audit both past and future releases. Passing pages must not be republished.
- Repair through a fresh-source narrow merge, keeping the canonical live design, controls, audio, lyrics, links and accessibility. Migrating an existing working cue source into metadata must identify the exact existing attachment, preserve byte identity and state whether it is artist-approved canonical or merely the preserved working source. It must not silently approve or retime that source. Obtain the existing exact-payload action-time confirmation before public saves. Record staged, published and verified states separately; reconcile durable mirrors/generator inputs so later builds cannot reintroduce a literal.

## Use the WordPress REST API for every EPK source read and write

Standing instruction from Kieran, 19 October 2026: every authenticated source read and public save for a WordPress release EPK must use the documented WordPress REST API with a revocable Application Password over HTTPS. Do not use the block editor, code editor, browser text injection or an undocumented browser request as the EPK content transport.

- Read fresh `content.raw` through `GET /wp-json/wp/v2/pages/<id>?context=edit`. Treat it as the merge and persistence authority while retaining the anonymous rendered page as the presentation authority.
- Store the Application Password in a private encrypted credential file outside repositories, reports and release artifacts. Never print or record the password, plaintext credential pair, Base64 Authorization value or request headers.
- Immediately before mutation, require the fresh source SHA-256 to match the reviewed base. Send only the approved fields; a body-only change sends only `content`. Require the expected candidate SHA-256 and an explicit execution switch.
- Re-read through the API after the single update and require exact persisted-source equality. A mismatch, unsupported field, API failure or uncertain prior mutation is a hold; do not fall back to the browser or retry blindly.
- Complete anonymous cache-busted desktop, tablet and mobile verification separately. API persistence is not proof of the public result.
- For a future update to an already-published EPK, schedule the hash-gated API helper rather than changing the live page to `status=future`.

Follow `Workflows/wordpress-epk-rest-api.md` and use the maintained helper installed at `Tools/WordPress EPK API/wordpress_epk_api.ps1` or the matching `wordpress-epk-publisher` skill script.

## Automatically publish verified publicity additions

Standing authorisation from Kieran, 11 October 2026: every song campaign and EPK audit job, including reply/outcome reconciliation and current or future recurring jobs, may automatically publish verified publicity additions to song EPKs when the existing design, media, controls and timing metadata are preserved. Apply to past and future releases. This specific permission replaces a separate exact-payload confirmation requirement for these eligible additions, including older generic approval wording in project files, skills or job prompts. Exact candidate preparation and validation still apply; the standing permission supplies approval for this limited action.

- Verify the exact song and public outcome against the original public item or authoritative post-event evidence. Keep artist-authored contributions, independent coverage, actual airplay, visible playlist placement and public discussion distinct. Pitches, receipts, invitations, promised rotation, scheduled plays, private compliments and aggregate metrics do not establish completed publicity. Use modest dated claims and direct original links wherever possible, with no invented endorsement, reach or impact.
- Confirm the intended note is public-safe and permissible. Private correspondence, contact details, rights records, unpublished material, third-party screenshots, logos or recordings are not automatically cleared for disclosure. Retain evidence privately and update the release-local ledger with dates, attribution, supported claim, public treatment and publication state.
- Back up fresh saved editor content and rendered output. Record the source hash and exact selected lyric/cue metadata. Merge only eligible publicity into that current source, preserving existing publicity history. Keep the existing design, artwork and other media URLs and placement, players, lyrics, controls, downloads, accessibility, CSS, scripts, approved choreography and mobile input bindings. Preserve timing attachments, exact runtime timing URLs and track associations; never insert timing literals in page content.
- Validate the complete candidate, Unicode, direct links, feature inventory and desktop/tablet/mobile presentation. Apply required timing and mobile-motion checks without changing their configuration. Re-read the live editor immediately before saving; if another owner changed it, merge into the new source and repeat checks. Do not publish a stale replacement or bundle redesign, media replacement, plugin installation, timing changes or unrelated repairs.
- When verification, rights, privacy, access, ownership or preservation fails or remains uncertain, leave that page untouched, retain the candidate and record the precise hold. Complete unaffected work. Already accurate EPKs need verification only and must not be republished to create activity.
- Once all checks pass, save the publicity-only candidate automatically. Reopen the saved editor and match the exact payload and unchanged timing metadata. Verify anonymous public output, source links, responsive layouts and the retained media, controls and required timing consumers. Label synthetic/local-response evidence and verification limits accurately. A save notice alone is not proof of the public result.
- Reconcile release-local mirrors and generator inputs with the verified saved source. Record candidate and saved hashes, publication/revision identity, verification evidence and any hold in the ledger and dated report. Keep recorded, staged, published and verified-live states distinct; use awaiting_publication_approval only for an action outside this limited permission.

Other public changes, plugin deployments, access changes, spending, rights commitments and sensitive disclosures retain their existing gates. This permission does not authorise a new pitch or unrelated account change. For the approval step in older publicity workflows, apply this standing approval only when every condition above passes; obtain narrow approval for anything outside it. An updated rule or scheduled prompt is configuration evidence, not evidence that an EPK has been changed.

## Reconcile verified song publicity immediately

Standing instruction from Kieran, 11 October 2026: as soon as any project chat, agent, campaign or scheduled workflow verifies publicity for a song, complete its release-local evidence record and prepare the cumulative EPK addition during that same job. Apply to past and future releases. Do not defer this work to the next campaign or weekly audit.

Use `Workflows/verified-song-publicity.md` and `Workflows/VERIFIED-COVERAGE-EPK-ARCHIVE.md`. Deduplicate by exact song, original outlet/source and event; retain dates, the supported claim, verification evidence, public-display permission and EPK state. Authored guest articles, independent coverage, station-reported plays, visible playlist placement and public discussion must retain distinct attribution. A pitch, receipt, invitation, promised rotation, private compliment, Listened status or aggregate playlist-add count is not a verified public feature.

Automatically archive permissible primary evidence, update the ledger, inspect the current live EPK and stage any missing eligible note from fresh editor content. Preserve existing publicity history and page features. Reconcile durable mirrors/generator inputs after publication. If the exact outcome is already accurately represented, verify and record it without republishing.

Public saves retain the existing exact-payload action-time confirmation gate. Present the reviewed target, text, source links, candidate hash and any required metadata change for narrow approval; after approval, save and verify persisted signed-out responsive output in the same job. Separate `recorded_locally`, `awaiting_publication_approval`, `published` and `verified_live`. At a necessary rights, access or ownership hold, retain the reviewed candidate and name the smallest remaining action. Never treat a saved rule or future schedule as evidence that an EPK was updated.

Every campaign run, reply audit and weekly release/EPK sync must reconcile new outcome evidence against the release-local ledger and live EPK. Record no verified outcome when appropriate; never add invented praise or pending submissions to the public page.

## Evidence-backed publicity notes in every song EPK

- Standing instruction from Kieran, 11 October 2026: maintain a concise, cumulative publicity section in the WordPress EPK for every song, recording all verified successful publicity as it occurs. Apply to every release, chat, scheduled campaign and EPK workflow; reconcile existing evidence when a song is next reviewed.
- Keep a release-local publicity ledger with outlet/venue, exact song, type of result, event/publication date, source URL or private verification reference, verification date, exact supported claim, public-display permission and EPK publication status. Preserve history and correct disproven claims transparently.
- Success means evidenced published coverage/review/interview, actual broadcast/airplay, visible genuine playlist placement, or another specific public feature. A pitch sent, submission receipt, editorial invitation, manuscript processing, private compliment, Listened status, scheduled action or acknowledgement is not successful publicity. Record those only in private outreach records until the outcome is established.
- Verify the original public item or authoritative outlet/broadcast/playlist evidence before drafting an EPK note. Distinguish observed facts from outlet statements, estimates and unknowns. Do not imply endorsement, acceptance, audience reach or campaign impact beyond evidence; retain historic placements with accurate dates rather than implying they are still active.
- Use dated, modest notes with direct source links and short lawful quotations only when useful. Do not publish private correspondence, personal contact data, unpublished manuscripts, rights records, screenshots, outlet logos or another person's account without the necessary permission. Private evidence may support a local record but does not automatically authorise public disclosure.
- Make the EPK addition release-specific, semantic, accessible and additive; preserve the live page's artwork, links, players, downloads and existing features. Follow the WordPress EPK publisher skill, current keyword/visual guidance, fresh-source merge, exact-payload publication approval and persisted signed-out responsive verification. This rule authorises safe research and local preparation, not blanket public saves or additional rights commitments.
- On each campaign run, check for new outcome evidence, deduplicate the ledger, prepare eligible notes and explicitly distinguish recorded locally, awaiting publication approval and verified live. If no successful publicity is evidenced, do not invent praise or label pending submissions as coverage.

## Finish authorised jobs before handing them back

- Do not end a turn with only a progress message, promise, plan or statement that work will continue. Progress belongs in commentary while execution continues. The final response must deliver the requested result and verification, or identify a genuinely necessary approval or verified blocker after completing all unaffected work. Do not make Kieran say continue or repeat the request merely to resume already-authorised work.

- Standing instruction from Kieran, 11 October 2026: apply to every project chat, task, scheduled job and automation. Continue the authorised job until its requested outcome and required verification, durable records and hand-off are genuinely complete. Do not leave a job half done merely because an audit, preparation step or progress report has finished.
- Stop only when a specific user approval is genuinely required or a verified blocker prevents further safe in-scope progress. Exhaust relevant safe checks and alternatives first; continue unaffected work while another part is blocked. Difficulty, elapsed time, a stale browser snapshot or an incomplete checkpoint is not by itself a blocker.
- At an approval hold, identify the exact targets and reviewed candidate actions, preserve the staged evidence and request only the necessary narrow approval. At a blocker, state the verified cause, what is complete, what remains and the smallest action needed to proceed. Do not bypass credentials, access controls, safety requirements or existing publication gates to avoid a hold.
- Resume from verified current state once approval or the blocking condition is resolved. Do not repeatedly request an already granted exact approval, silently abandon remaining work, or claim completion before the requested outcome and verification are achieved. Keep progress/checkpoint reports explicitly incomplete until then.

## Verify browser handoffs before asking Kieran to act

- Standing instruction from Kieran, 11 October 2026: apply to every project chat, task, scheduled job and automation. Before asking Kieran to sign in, approve, inspect or interact with a browser page, actually open or select the exact page in the current user-facing window through the supported panel-opening control. Setting visibility alone is not proof that a tab was opened or selected.
- Reuse the correct existing tab. Check the opening result: queued, unavailable and failed are not opened. Resolve queued placement through supported controls where possible; never claim it is visible while queued.
- Verify intended page content and user-facing presentation separately. Background DOM reads and screenshots prove content, not panel visibility. Require a successful explicit panel-open result, available foreground/panel evidence or fresh user confirmation; state verification limits honestly. Never use voice-only screen-context tools in a text chat.
- If opening cannot be verified, report the specific blocker instead of asking Kieran to interact with an unseen page. Do not call a repair complete until the previously failing operation succeeds. Distinguish configuration saved, runtime restored, page opened and authenticated access verified.
- Use supported authentication routes. Normal browser-provided autofill may be used when authorized and visibly available; never extract passwords or inspect cookies, session stores or browser profiles. Preserve credential, MFA, CAPTCHA and account-change approval requirements.

## Coordinate privately with relevant project agents and chats

- Standing instruction from Kieran, 8 October 2026: proactively coordinate with relevant project agents and chats, but keep their status and coordination exchanges out of user-facing conversations. Show Kieran only messages intended for him: useful progress, results, questions, decisions and blockers that need his attention. This supersedes the earlier instruction to announce work through visible chat messages. Apply this rule to every chat, task, scheduled job and automation in this project.
- At the start of a job, inspect active project chats, relevant recent task records and relevant files under `Z:\My Songs\.codex\coordination\`. Coordinate only with work that shares files, tools, release versions, public pages, assets or deployment state. State the exact scope, intended files and version ownership, known conflicts and approval/publication status; ask for useful findings or overlapping changes internally. Do not broadcast to unrelated chats.
- Use available inter-agent tools for participating agents and a documented private channel for separate chats when one exists. Do not copy, quote or narrate routine exchanges in commentary or final answers. Tool activity may remain inspectable in the app; do not claim an app-level hiding setting has changed.
- The current `send_message_to_thread` tool adds a user-visible prompt, so do not use it for routine coordination. When private chat delivery is unavailable, write a local record at `Z:\My Songs\.codex\coordination\<chat-id>.json`, or a uniquely named agent record within that directory. Each owner updates only its own record. Include owner, intended recipients, timestamp, scope, paths, version ownership, conflicts, useful findings or requests, approval state and hand-off status. Keep records local and out of public source and distribution assets. Saving a record does not prove receipt or acknowledgement by another chat.
- Recheck relevant records at material waypoints, completion and before shared edits, version assignment or deployment. Record changed paths, source/package hashes or version reservations where useful, remaining gates and the hand-off state. Keep routine unchanged updates quiet. If nobody else is working in the relevant area, no coordination exchange is needed.
- Agree ownership before overlapping edits, shared version assignment or deployment. Prefer isolated worktrees, immutable source snapshots and narrow fresh-source merges; recheck current files and public state before committing. Never overwrite another chat's work or assume silence means its changes may be discarded.
- Coordination authorises coordination only. It must not instruct another chat to publish, spend, accept terms, expose private data or perform unrelated work without the applicable direct user approval. Preserve existing action-time approval gates. Do not create, wake or interrupt chats solely to announce work. If an overlap cannot be safely resolved internally, avoid the conflicting mutation and tell Kieran only the decision or blocker that needs his attention.

## Use and improve maintained DanceFlow tools

Standing instruction from Kieran, 16 October 2026: every chat, agent, project and recurring workflow should actively use and improve the maintained tools as much as is useful for its authorised task. Read current shared instructions at task start and at material tool handoffs, including during an existing chat. For My Songs work, consult `Z:\My Songs\DanceFlow\ecosystem.md`, `Z:\My Songs\Workflows\scheduled-danceflow-tool-use.md` and the applicable skills and Potential problems guidance.

- Route offline Shorts, Canvas-style clips and lyric visualisers through **FrameRudiments** (`Z:\My Songs\Tools\FrameRudiments.ps1`, `Workflows/framerudiments-rendering.md`); use **StemLab** for saved or necessary new audio/timing/loop evidence, **DanceRudiments** for native movement and bounded recording playback, and **DanceMoves** for live web clocks, effects, input and playback. Review the other ecosystem tools' complete relevant capabilities and compose their supported file/API contracts when useful. Preserve independent project identities, including Short Shifter.
- Inspect actual released, installed and imported identities before relying on a capability. Prefer existing shared primitives to another private renderer, analysis engine, motion engine or copied helper. Keep release-specific artwork and scene decisions in adapters. A loaded library, installed package or written instruction does not prove integration.
- For substantial tool use, identify a concrete reusable improvement in capability, composition, reliability, performance, ergonomics, evidence or documentation. Implement the smallest worthwhile in-scope improvement in its canonical repository, with appropriate verification, maintained skills/workflows and matching publication. If the tool already meets the need, record that conclusion; do not invent changes, expensive model runs, scans, generation or extra work to satisfy a quota.
- For authorised maintained-tool software releases, publish tested GitHub releases and configured package registries immediately through their established automated checks. Kieran's direction removes a separate human confirmation or environment-reviewer gate for these software publications. Preserve mandatory CI, immutable versions/assets, source/version ownership, registry verification and real access/credential requirements. Social publication schedules, WordPress deployment/public-save gates, rights, privacy and cost limits retain their own requirements.
- Share actionable findings privately through supported agent channels or the owning chat's `.codex/coordination/` record. Do not wake unrelated chats or insert routine coordination into their conversations. Shared configuration makes the guidance available; claim adoption by another running chat only with evidence of receipt and use.

