Read `Workflows/ecosystem-tool-workflows.md` for the current tool's task, inputs, outputs, acceptance and limits, together with its exact versioned native capability reference. Read `Workflows/tool-development-lessons.md` when development or substantial use exposes a reusable gap. Recover the user decision and observed failure from available ChatGPT/Codex history, verify against current source and a relevant case, then choose an owning-tool fix, shared workflow update, project adapter, existing-capability conclusion or precise hold. Keep incomplete history explicit and private transcripts out of public packages. Preserve original data and prior outputs, exact source/package/clock identity, native-host qualification and actual visual/consumer acceptance. Run the released WorkflowMoves workflow-coverage checker after membership/routes change; its structural pass does not establish tool invocation, exhaustive history review or adoption by another running chat.

## Make full use of the project tool library

- Apply this rule to every chat, task, scheduled job and automation in this project.
- Before designing, changing or troubleshooting a workflow, inspect the relevant existing project tools, libraries, skills and workflows, including their current documentation, capability inventory and reusable primitives. Review the full relevant functionality rather than assuming a familiar entry point is the only available feature.
- Always try to use and improve the project tools to the fullest extent applicable to the task. This includes StemLab, KeywordMoves, DanceMoves, DanceRudiments, RasterMoves, LikenessForge, PixelCue, ThumbMoves, React Timeline Sequence, Short Shifter, EPK Social Metadata and other maintained project tools. Check the current feature inventory, documentation, source, tests and supported integrations before choosing an approach; do not assume a familiar entry point represents the whole tool.
- Preserve each tool's project identity. Short Shifter is entirely unrelated to DanceFlow; keep it out of DanceFlow membership lists, diagrams, copy and structured data. The DanceFlow component inventory is `Z:\My Songs\DanceFlow\ecosystem.md`. LikenessForge is the separately installed facial-likeness repair and identity-LoRA tool; follow `Workflows/likenessforge-image-preparation.md` for reviewed image handoffs. Short Shifter remains covered by the general project-tool rules as an independent project.
- Prefer supported capabilities and composition over bespoke scripts, duplicated engines or page-local replacements. When a useful required feature is missing, add it to the appropriate shared tool during the same job, with tests, documentation and a reproducible example, and verify it before relying on a separate implementation. Record any genuine blocker or reason a proposed feature does not belong in that tool.
- For all web motion work, including the ordinary site's shared templates as well as EPKs, review DanceMoves' complete applicable functionality: effects and rendering primitives, clocks and timing, input and orientation, scheduling, visibility and playback lifecycle, adaptive quality downgrade and recovery, reduced motion and forced colours, cleanup and teardown, diagnostics and validation. Connect the required owners and controllers explicitly; loading DanceMoves alone does not activate those features.
- Verify that each relevant capability is actually registered, connected and operating in the rendered result. Exercise downgrade and recovery, preference overrides and lifecycle changes where motion is involved; configuration or a loaded library is not evidence of working integration.
- Use the capabilities that serve the task and preserve its creative intent, accessibility, performance and evidence requirements. Record any applicable capability deliberately omitted, its reason and the verification limits. Do not add effects solely to exercise every API, or claim unsupported functionality is active.
- Give every substantial use of a project tool an explicit improvement goal. Identify at least one concrete opportunity to improve capability, composition, evidence, ergonomics, reliability, performance or documentation; implement the smallest worthwhile reusable improvement during the same job when safe, or record why the reviewed tool already meets the need and no change is justified. Immediate delivery and tool improvement are both completion criteria when a reusable gap is found.

## Push project updates to GitHub before hand-off

- Standing instruction from Kieran, 11 October 2026: always push completed project source, tool, skill and workflow updates to their established GitHub repository during the same job. Local edits, staged files, commits and version bumps are incomplete until the remote branch contains the exact validated changes and its commit identity has been read back. Apply to every project chat, task, scheduled job and automation.
- Before changing a Git checkout, inspect its branch, remote, staged and unstaged changes and relevant ownership records. Preserve unrelated work. Use an isolated branch or checkout when ownership is mixed; never push another owner's changes, private song material, credentials, personal data or rights records merely to empty a dirty working tree.
- Before hand-off, reconcile the job's changed paths with the pushed commit, PR and required checks. Follow the existing owned-PR merge, matching-release and registry-publication rules where applicable. A push is not proof of a merge, release or registry acceptance.
- If no appropriate repository exists, resolve a suitable GitHub destination within the authorised scope before treating reusable source or workflow updates as finished. Keep private operational records separate from publishable source. For a genuine access, ownership, privacy or validation blocker, preserve the reviewed candidate and record the exact unpushed paths, reason and smallest remaining action; do not call the update complete or silently carry it into another job.
- When finding older local changes, compare them with current remote source and published releases. Identify already-published equivalents, genuinely unpublished work and unresolved ownership separately. Never reset, discard or blindly publish an old mixed working tree.

## Every project tool improvement requires GitHub publication and a release

- Standing authorisation from Kieran, 8 October 2026: automatically merge our own improvement PRs in repositories we own once all required CI and applicable integration/deployment tests pass for the exact current PR revision. Do not stop for another general merge confirmation. Recheck repository ownership, source/version ownership, the current PR head, required-check conclusions and unresolved review or source conflicts immediately before merging. Never bypass a failed, pending or required check. Third-party repositories retain their maintainers' review and merge authority.
- Standing instruction from Kieran, 8 October 2026: registry submissions and publication must run automatically through the established GitHub workflows after a release is tagged, once the required validation and exact source/package identity checks pass. Prefer trusted publishing and scoped credentials, publish the same tested artifacts, preserve immutable versions and independently verify each destination. Add a missing supported registry route to the workflow rather than relying on manual uploads. Initial account setup, registry approval, credentials or destination eligibility may remain external gates; record the exact unavailable route and retained candidate without claiming successful submission or listing. A tagged release or GitHub upload is not evidence that a registry accepted it.
- After an authorised merge, continue through the matching release and configured registry workflows in the same job. Verify the exact merged source, fresh tag, tested distributions, checksums and registry readback. Merge and automation do not authorise unrelated deployment, spending, changes to access or publication of private material.


- Standing instruction from Kieran, 8 October 2026: apply this rule to every chat, task, scheduled job and automation in this project, and to every maintained project tool, library, plugin and reusable workflow package.
- Whenever an improvement is made, push the completed, validated changes to the tool's established GitHub repository and publish a new matching GitHub release during the same job. This includes useful improvements to features, integrations, reliability, performance, evidence, tests, packaging and maintained documentation. A local edit, version bump, build, pushed commit or tag alone is not completion.
- Identify the exact source commit/tag, choose a fresh version, run the tool's applicable checks, and use its established build and publication workflow. Include tested installable distributions and checksums where applicable, describe the changes and verification, and independently verify the published release and asset identity. Verify each configured package-registry destination separately rather than inferring it from GitHub publication.
- Preserve immutable published tags and assets, existing rollback packages and other chats' ownership. Keep private song material, account data, credentials and rights evidence out of public source and packages. Follow applicable destination, privacy, rights and approval requirements; this completion rule does not authorise unrelated deployments, spending or changes to access.
- If validation or publication is blocked, retain the reviewed candidate and record the exact partial state, failure evidence and outstanding publication task. Do not call the improvement finished or silently carry an unreleased version into successor work. Apply any stricter tool-specific publication or pre-deployment gate as well.

## Every DanceMoves version requires a GitHub release

- A verified matching published GitHub release is a pre-deployment gate, not a retrospective documentation task. Do not install a new DanceMoves version on WordPress while its matching release is absent. Local candidates must be labelled unpublished and must not be described as releases. When a missing publication is discovered, stop successor release advancement and reconcile that exact deployed version first.
- Apply this rule to every chat, task, scheduled job and automation in this project. Whenever a new DanceMoves version is created, always publish a matching GitHub release in `https://github.com/kieransimkin/DanceMoves` as part of that same version's workflow, before declaring the version or tool work complete. A local version bump, package build, tag or WordPress installation alone does not satisfy this rule.
- Release the exact validated version from an identified source commit/tag. Attach the tested distribution packages and checksum manifest, including the exact WordPress ZIP when applicable, and document changes and verification. Verify the published release/version, asset availability and hashes independently against the local tested packages and any deployed WordPress package.
- Preserve immutable published tags/assets and the previous rollback package. Do not publish dirty or unidentified source, overwrite a released version with different bytes, or infer successful GitHub publication from a website installation. If publication is blocked or only part of the workflow succeeds, record the partial state and outstanding release task explicitly; do not report completion.
- This completion rule preserves the applicable repository, privacy and action-time approval requirements. Keep private song, account and rights evidence out of public release assets. Reconcile an existing version discovered without a matching release before extending it; do not silently carry the gap into another version.

## Spotify Canvas follows the song's Shorts generator

- Standing authorization from Kieran, 8 October 2026: when Kieran approves a specific Canvas upload/replacement, that approval also authorises accepting the previously disclosed Spotify Canvas rights declaration, licence and indemnity on his behalf. Do not ask for a second terms approval. Check the current terms for material changes; flag any new commitment outside the disclosed scope. Exact candidate/track approval, content checks and persisted verification still apply.

- Every new or revised Canvas must include the exact canonical song title and artist name as readable, restrained, track-relevant artwork text. This is Kieran's project preference, not a Spotify requirement. Apply to every song, release, chat, scheduled job and automation. Keep imagery primary; check both text elements' contrast, phone crop, motion extremes and live player overlays. Do not add promotional links, calls to follow/buy, unrelated branding or sung-lyric captions.
- Refresh Spotify's official Canvas guidelines, content policy and uploader requirements before preparing or uploading a Canvas. Distinguish mandatory specifications/content rules from optional best-practice recommendations. As checked 8 October 2026, Spotify recommends considering excluding titles but permits relevant supporting text; this project deliberately includes the title and artist name. If future mandatory policy conflicts, flag the conflict and prepare a compliant alternative rather than silently disregarding either rule. Follow the dated evidence and QA checklist in `Workflows/spotify-upcoming-canvas.md`. Audit existing Canvases for missing titles when next inspected; prepare versioned replacements and preserve exact-file upload/terms confirmation requirements.

- Apply this rule to every song, release, chat, task, scheduled job and automation in this project, including already released music and upcoming releases. Follow `Z:\My Songs\Workflows\spotify-upcoming-canvas.md`.
- Always use the song's established Shorts generator when one exists. Resolve the exact release-local generator, version, settings and visual inputs from its provenance records; do not substitute static artwork for convenience or silently choose a different renderer. If its identity or approval is uncertain, record that uncertainty and resolve it rather than claiming it approved.
- Whenever a song gains an approved Shorts generator, or an approved material revision, update its Spotify Canvas as part of that workflow. Inspect the current persisted Canvas and its source lineage first. Prepare a dedicated Canvas derivative from the approved generator; refresh a static fallback, an older generator-derived Canvas or a missing Canvas. If the current Canvas already matches the current approved generator, verify and record it without a duplicate upload.
- Adapt the generator for Spotify's current Canvas specifications and independent playback: preserve the song's visual identity, remove inappropriate sung-lyric captions, lip-sync, promotional links and Shorts UI, and verify the exact final file, loop, first frame, phone crop and applicable safety checks. Preserve the original Shorts, generator and previous Canvas assets. Record generator approval evidence, source version/hashes, candidate hash, target track and refresh status in the release-local manifest or ledger.
- Use a generated static 9:16 extension of the canonical cover only when no established Shorts generator exists. Mark it as a fallback that is due for replacement once a Shorts generator is approved; do not pad the unchanged square cover instead of the authorised generated extension.
- Complete all safe local preparation without waiting for another general instruction. Before uploading or replacing a Canvas, obtain any mandatory action-time confirmation for the exact candidate and Spotify track, including destination terms when required. Generator approval alone is not approval of a later unseen Canvas file or a rights/indemnity commitment. After the authorised save, reopen the manager and verify persistence; pre-release persistence is not proof of public visibility or listener/save uplift.

## Token-efficient execution choices

- Apply this rule to every chat, task, scheduled job and automation in this project.
- Before committing to a materially token-expensive approach, actively check for a lower-token method that can achieve the same verified outcome within the same safety, privacy, rights, approval and quality constraints.
- When a credible lower-token option exists, briefly offer it to Kieran before automatically implementing the more expensive default. State the practical trade-off clearly, including any reduction in isolation, coverage, fidelity, confidence or convenience. Do not imply that a cheaper option is equivalent when it is not.
- Prefer the lower-token option when Kieran selects it or has already established it as the standing preference. Do not use token savings to bypass required evidence, testing, approval gates, accessibility, security boundaries or destination policies.
- If the options are materially equivalent and the cheaper route introduces no meaningful new risk or loss of quality, use it without unnecessary ceremony and record the choice only when it affects reproducibility.

