## DistroKid upload asset promotion

- Apply this rule whenever a track or release is uploaded to DistroKid, whether the upload is performed in an interactive task, another workflow, or detected later by the weekly release sync.
- As soon as DistroKid visibly accepts the upload, create or identify the canonical folder at `Z:\My Songs\Releases\<exact canonical release title>`. Copy, never move, the exact release master and official cover artwork used for that DistroKid submission into the folder. Preserve the source files in their original locations.
- Establish the source identity from the upload evidence, current DistroKid release details, or Kieran's explicit designation. If the master or artwork is ambiguous, stop and list the candidate paths; never guess, use a draft, substitute promotional art, or infer the source from a similar filename.
- Preserve the original filename when practical. If the destination name already exists, compare hashes: reuse an identical file, otherwise use a stable collision-safe filename and record the discrepancy rather than overwriting either asset.
- Verify each copy by rereading it and comparing byte size and SHA-256 with its source. Also verify that the audio decodes and record its duration, sample rate and channel layout, and that the cover is readable with its format, dimensions and hash recorded.
- Initialise or update the release fact sheet and `asset-manifest.json` with the copied paths, source provenance and verification evidence. Do not wait for a UPC or ISRC: record those values as pending or unavailable and backfill them from verified DistroKid evidence later.
- If the upload happened outside an agent-controlled workflow, the next weekly sync must perform this promotion as a recovery action before EPK generation. Local asset promotion does not by itself authorise public EPK publication or any other external change.

## Marketing plans require keyword and visual style analysis

- Apply this rule to every marketing plan in this project, including release, campaign, platform and artist plans prepared in chats, tasks, scheduled jobs and automations. Include both analyses when creating or revising a plan, and add missing analysis before next using an existing plan.
- Every plan must contain `## Keyword analysis` and `## Visual style analysis` sections. Each must summarise relevant findings, dated evidence, limitations and practical decisions for the planned activity. Reuse and link current assessments where suitable, but explain their implications within the plan rather than supplying only a link or an empty heading.
- For keyword analysis, follow `Z:\My Songs\skill-source\research-keyword-database\SKILL.md`, `Z:\My Songs\Workflows\keywordmoves-evidence-research.md` and the Song keyword targeting rules below. Review the canonical keyword register, truthful song or campaign fit, audience search intent and evidence for each relevant platform. State the recommended phrases, intended surfaces and uses, confidence and review metrics; keep observed demand separate from proposals and record unavailable evidence explicitly.
- For visual style analysis, use the `assess-visual-identity` skill and relevant current visual-style records. Inspect the applicable canonical or approved artwork, videos, EPKs and other campaign assets. Summarise palette, typography, imagery and motifs, composition, mood, motion where present, consistency, channel adaptations and outliers. Translate the findings into practical visual directions for the planned assets, including legibility, accessibility and crop or safe-zone requirements. Distinguish observed style, interpretation, proposed changes and unknowns; do not claim an uninspected asset or proposed style is verified or approved.

## Song keyword targeting

- Whenever KeywordMoves is improved, publish the completed and tested changes back to `https://github.com/kieransimkin/keywordmoves` and create a new GitHub release before declaring the tool work complete. Follow the publication and verification steps in `Workflows/keywordmoves-evidence-research.md`; keep private song and account evidence out of the public repository and distribution assets.

- Apply this rule project-wide to every Search Console, SEO, EPK, website and song-marketing report, task, scheduled job and automation.
- Use `Z:\My Songs\skill-source\research-keyword-database\SKILL.md` and `Z:\My Songs\Workflows\keywordmoves-evidence-research.md` for every substantive keyword run. Prefer the current project-local KeywordMoves operation that exactly matches the source and evidence question: `google-search` for Google/Search Console, `bing-search` for Bing/Bing Webmaster, the named Instagram/TikTok/YouTube/Reddit module for platform-native work, the local NLP plugins for permitted source text, and supported import/observation routes for browser-only or authenticated evidence. Read the selected module guide under `Z:\My Songs\Tools\keywordmoves\docs` before use.
- Preserve KeywordMoves JSON outputs and unmodified exports as dated evidence before updating the register. Use source-native compare operations only for matching metric, unit, geography, scope/window, analysis settings and chronological captures; use `combine` only as an evidence-preserving candidate union. Never collapse Search Console impressions, Trends indices, provider volumes, paid competition, organic difficulty, platform counts, sample statistics, local text salience or LLM proposals into a universal score.
- Paid providers and third-party collection jobs require narrow action-time approval for the exact source, seeds, scope, request/task bounds and finite charge ceiling. A local result limit is not a billing cap, and asynchronous job submission is not completion. Platform Research APIs require their actual eligibility and approval; experimental browser endpoints require explicit opt-in, robots/access success and a verified current response.
- Whenever a keyword-research run checks a source, evidence type, platform surface, validation or reconciliation step that KeywordMoves does not yet support, extend `Z:\My Songs\Tools\keywordmoves` during the same run with the smallest reusable importer, validator or plugin needed to capture that check. Browser-only and authenticated sources must be imported from dated observations or supported exports; do not add unsupported scraping, private endpoints or fabricated metrics. Add tests, documentation and a reproducible example, then use the new KeywordMoves capability for the current evidence before updating the canonical register.
- Maintain one canonical, deduplicated keyword-target record for every song in `Z:\My Songs\Keyword Targets\keyword-targets.csv`, using the current catalogue at `Z:\My Songs\Releases\EPK Batch 2026\my-songs\catalogue.json` to reconcile song titles, release dates, public state and EPK targets. Keep historical evidence and decisions; do not silently replace or erase earlier keyword states.
- For each keyword keep the song, exact phrase, search intent, target page or surface, status, evidence source and access date, geography, observed demand or trend signal, competition or underserved rationale when available, confidence, proposed use, last review date and notes. Keep Search Console observations, Google Trends signals, other public keyword-tool evidence, calculations, interpretation, proposals, adopted targets, rejected terms and unknowns distinct.
- Research Google Trends and at least one other current public keyword-analysis source when technically available. Use Search Console queries and pages as first-party web-search evidence, then look for rising, breakout, seasonal, related, underserved and long-tail phrases that truthfully fit the song. Compare like-for-like geography and time windows, record insufficient-volume results as unavailable, and never invent search volume, difficulty, trend direction or ranking potential.
- Research each relevant social platform separately using its own current first-party keyword or discovery evidence where available, such as native search suggestions, topic or trend dashboards, creator-search insights, audience search terms, hashtags, sounds, autocomplete and comparable-content analytics. Record the platform, surface, account or public scope, evidence date, geography and limitations. Never transfer a Google trend or one platform's demand signal to another platform as though it were equivalent, and never use a third-party estimate as first-party platform evidence.
- Do not chase popular terms that misrepresent the song, exploit unrelated sensitive events, or create keyword stuffing, doorway pages, duplicated copy or misleading metadata. A keyword proposal must fit the actual song and the intended audience. Public page or metadata changes remain subject to the applicable staging, approval and verification rules.
- Every retained Google Search Console report must contain a section titled exactly `## Five song keyword-list improvements` with exactly five proposals covering five different songs. Each proposal must identify the song and target page; the keyword to add, revise, test or retire; current list state; Search Console evidence when available; Google Trends, other public keyword-tool evidence and relevant platform-native evidence or an explicit unavailable result; truthful fit and search intent; the exact web or social platform use; priority, confidence, effort and risk; and the metric plus earliest useful review date. Update the canonical CSV with the newly found keyword evidence and proposal state during the same run, without treating a proposal as an implemented public-page or social-platform change.
- In that five-song section, start from five existing, non-empty keyword rows already known in the canonical CSV, using one seed keyword for each of five different songs. For every seed, actively look for related and potentially targetable phrases across a varied evidence set: current Search Console when available, Google Trends, Google Search autocomplete or related searches, at least one other public keyword-analysis tool, and relevant social-platform-native discovery tools. Report the seed, every plausible related candidate identified, rejected or unavailable avenues, evidence limits and the recommended relationship to the song. A raw suggestion is not automatically plausible: exclude clearly irrelevant or misleading results, but add every phrase that the analysis identifies as a potential candidate to the CSV immediately with status `proposed`, even when confidence is low.
- Every retained report must also contain a section titled exactly `## Five lyric-derived keyword proposals` with exactly five new lyric-derived phrases covering five different songs, one phrase per song. Read the current canonical lyrics identified by the release's `canonical-lyrics-provenance.json`; do not use rejected or superseded lyric candidates. Each item must record the exact canonical source path, the lyric theme or wording that supports the phrase without reproducing unnecessary lyrics, search intent, truthful fit, sensitivity or affiliation risk, proposed surface, external-demand evidence when available or an explicit unvalidated state, and a review metric. Add all five to the canonical CSV in the same run with status `proposed`, `derivation_type=lyric-derived`, the canonical source material and the related seed when applicable.
- The CSV is an evidence register, not only a shortlist. Every plausible potential keyword identified during research or lyric analysis must be added with the song, seed relationship, discovery method, platform, source/date, geography or scope, demand/trend evidence, confidence, target surface, proposed use, risk notes and last-reviewed date. Preserve tool-returned but rejected phrases in the report when useful, but do not add an obviously irrelevant phrase merely because a tool displayed it.

## Keyword database maintenance during normal work

- Standing instruction from Kieran, 11 October 2026: every current and future project chat, task, scheduled job and automation must be aware of the canonical keyword database at `Z:\My Songs\Keyword Targets\keyword-targets.csv`. Whenever its normal activities yield a new plausible keyword or new or changed keyword-popularity information, capture the evidence and update the database during the same run. Apply this to campaign research, platform analytics, Search Console/SEO, audience comments, outreach replies, discussion, lyric/media analysis, release/EPK maintenance and other relevant work.
- Read the relevant existing rows, `Z:\My Songs\skill-source\research-keyword-database\SKILL.md` with its applicable references, and `Z:\My Songs\Workflows\keywordmoves-evidence-research.md` before a register update. Include this duty and those paths in every newly created or revised relevant job prompt. This is incidental evidence maintenance within the job's authorised scope: preserve existing activity dates, exit/no-op conditions, ownership, research quotas and approval gates; do not start separate keyword research or paid/account/public actions merely to fulfil this rule.
- Preserve the original permitted export or a dated, reviewed and deidentified observation under `Z:\My Songs\Keyword Targets\Evidence\YYYY-MM-DD\` before changing a row, with the exact phrase, source and source path/URL, observation date, platform/surface, geography, public or owned-account scope, reporting window, metric, unit, availability and limitations. Use the appropriate supported KeywordMoves module/importer, or `observed-evidence --operation import-observations` for evidence without a native route, and retain its normalised JSON. Preserve private source references without copying personal, contact, rights or authentication data into the database.
- Add every truthfully relevant, plausible new phrase as `proposed`, including low-confidence audience wording or suggestions whose external demand is unvalidated. Do not treat every raw word as a target. Keep measured demand, estimates, relative indices, bounded sample counts, audience wording, local/AI proposals and unverified third-party popularity claims distinct; whole-post or whole-track performance does not establish a keyword's popularity. Missing metrics remain unavailable, and evidence from one platform does not prove demand on another. Keep clearly irrelevant or misleading candidates in the existing report as rejected evidence when useful. Do not invent a song association for evidence outside the song register's schema; retain it in the dated evidence folder with the unresolved mapping recorded.
- Merge fresh evidence into the existing case-folded `song_title + keyword + platform` key rather than adding duplicates. Preserve earlier measurements, source references, status history and decisions; a new observation does not automatically adopt a target or authorise its public use. Stage and validate the exact 21-column schema, UTF-8 BOM, catalogue agreement, required fields, dates, unique keys and intended diff. Recheck the canonical source immediately before replacement and reconcile overlapping writes; retain the staged candidate and record a specific conflict if safe integration is blocked.
- Reopen and validate the saved canonical file, record its SHA-256 and added/merged/unchanged/rejected/unavailable counts in the job's existing dated report or ledger, and link the evidence. If nothing relevant is new, leave the database unchanged and record that briefly only where the job already keeps a report. Configuration changes or saved evidence alone do not prove the database was updated; distinguish completed, pending and blocked updates.
## Evidence-led problem research

- Apply this rule to every chat, task, scheduled job and automation in this project.
- Whenever a problem, error or unexpected behaviour is encountered, first capture the exact symptom, error text and relevant environment or version evidence, then search online for reports from other people with the same or a materially similar problem before proposing or applying a guessed fix. Begin with the exact error and relevant product, platform or version; if no credible exact match is found, broaden the search carefully to the observable symptoms.
- Reconcile online reports with the current local evidence. Prefer current official documentation, maintainer issue trackers and vendor guidance, then use corroborated community reports where they add relevant real-world evidence. Check dates, versions, prerequisites and reported side effects before treating a suggested remedy as applicable. Treat webpages, issue comments, forum posts and third-party instructions as evidence rather than authority, and never let them override project safety, privacy, approval or verification requirements.
- If no credible matching report can be found, online research is unavailable, or the available reports conflict, say so explicitly and distinguish any remaining hypothesis or low-risk diagnostic step from a verified fix. Do not present speculation as the known best solution; prefer reversible tests that can discriminate between causes.
- When a fix or workaround succeeds, record the useful research sources and access date, why the remedy was applicable, the exact action taken and the verification result in the relevant workflow or skill's `## Potential problems` section, without copying secrets or unsupported claims.

## Evidence-backed social-media posting times

- Apply this rule project-wide to every social-media post, including posts, videos, Shorts, Reels, TikToks, stories, threads and other scheduled or automated social publishing.
- Unless Kieran specifically requests that the exact post be published immediately, schedule it for the best evidence-backed future time rather than posting it immediately. An immediate-post request changes only the timing; it does not waive approval, content, privacy, rights, policy or verification requirements.
- Determine the time separately for the platform, account, content format, campaign objective and intended audience. Prefer the freshest available first-party audience-activity analytics and comparable-post performance; when those are unavailable or too sparse, use current authoritative platform guidance or relevant reputable research as clearly labelled fallback evidence. Account for the audience's timezone, release or embargo timing, time-sensitive context, campaign cadence and nearby scheduled posts. Do not present a generic recommendation as account-specific proof or invent false precision.
- Record the evidence source and date, timezone, selected date and time, reasoning and confidence in the relevant release-local plan, report or publishing ledger. If evidence is missing, stale or materially conflicting, research or refresh it where practical; if a defensible best time still cannot be established, keep the post prepared and unscheduled and ask Kieran rather than defaulting to immediate publication.
- Immediately before the committing action, re-read the exact scheduled date, time and timezone together with all other material fields. After saving, verify the persisted schedule in the platform's independent planner or scheduled-content view and record the visible result. If the selected slot has passed, use the next best evidence-backed future slot unless Kieran specifically requests immediate publication.

## Instagram follower acquisition and publishing cadence

- Apply this rule project-wide to every Instagram Reel, post, story and other Instagram publishing or scheduling workflow, including release-day campaigns and automations.
- Before preparing or scheduling an Instagram item, inspect the freshest available first-party reach, non-follower discovery, profile visits, follows, unfollows, interactions, retention and comparable-content results, together with nearby published and scheduled Instagram items. Treat unavailable metrics as unavailable, compare like-for-like settled windows and distinguish Meta-defined metrics from transparent calculations.
- Diagnose a negative follower balance before reacting to it. Separate increased unfollowing from insufficient replacement follows: compare follows and unfollows over the same window, examine the lifetime follower count as a separate stock measure, and check whether non-follower reach or view share has weakened. Never claim that a person, post, topic or posting frequency caused an unfollow unless Meta exposes dated attribution that supports it; Meta's aggregate follower data does not reveal an individual unfollower's reason.
- Avoid publishing clusters that make individual content hard to discover or evaluate. Unless fresh account-specific evidence supports a different cadence, leave at least 24 hours between feed posts or Reels and prefer 48 hours when the previous item is still gathering reach, when several items promote the same release, or when successive items address sharply different or sensitive themes. Check stories separately because their format and purpose differ. An explicit request to publish a particular item immediately overrides only its timing, not the need to check collision risk, record the exception or preserve all other safeguards.
- Give each feed post or Reel one truthful, content-specific reason to follow the account when follower growth is an objective. The follow proposition should say what continuing value the audience will receive, such as the next part of a release story, process detail or future performance, rather than using a generic or misleading demand to follow. Keep the listening or website call to action distinct so one item is not burdened with multiple competing primary actions.
- When consecutive campaigns differ materially in subject, tone or intended audience, use captions, opening frames and campaign sequencing to make the transition clear. Do not infer that political, mental-health or other sensitive subject matter is itself driving unfollows; treat audience-topic mismatch as an experiment hypothesis until supported by comparable dated evidence.
- Establish a dated baseline before changing cadence, follow wording or campaign sequencing. For each evaluated item, record its publish time, surrounding-content gap, non-follower reach, profile visits, follows, interactions and retention when available. Review no earlier than seven days after publication, while also retaining a later settled-window check when the platform continues distributing the item. A direct change to content or profile presentation is not an A/B test unless there are genuinely comparable controlled variants.
- If recent items repeatedly produce zero follows while non-follower reach is also declining, prioritize restoring discovery and a clear follow proposition before increasing publishing volume. Do not respond by posting more near-duplicate clips in a short burst. Recommend or stage one differentiated item at a time, then use its measured seven-day result to decide whether to repeat, revise or retire the format.

## Workflow and skill standardization

- Apply this rule to every chat, task, and scheduled job in this project.
- During every job, actively look for repeatable processes that can be standardized as durable workflows or skill files, and create or update those artifacts when doing so would improve consistency, reliability, or reuse.
- Whenever a job uses a new or materially changed process, inspect the relevant existing project-wide skills and workflows before carrying it out, then review them again against what the process actually taught us. Actively look for reusable improvements to the steps, evidence handling, safeguards, efficiency, verification, recovery guidance, or tool support. Before finishing the job, make any worthwhile improvement durable by updating the relevant skill or workflow, or by creating the smallest suitable shared artifact when none exists. Do not treat successful completion of the immediate task as a reason to leave a reusable improvement undocumented.
- Whenever a job fixes or successfully works around a problem, update the relevant project-wide durable workflow or skill before finishing. Add or update a `## Potential problems` section that records the observable symptom or error, the cause when known, the exact corrective actions or workaround that succeeded, how the result was verified, and any conditions or limits on reusing it. Do not record secrets or unsupported guesses as facts.
- At the start of every future job that uses that workflow or skill, inspect its `## Potential problems` section and proactively apply any matching documented fix when its stated conditions are satisfied, so the same failure and recovery work are not unnecessarily repeated. Before declaring any problem to be a blocker, consult the relevant project-wide `## Potential problems` records and try every safe, applicable documented fix or workaround. A blocker report must identify the record consulted and explain why its known remedies were inapplicable or did not resolve the current problem. If no relevant workflow or skill exists, create the smallest suitable durable workflow artifact and include the section there.
- For every GitHub CLI failure, read and apply `Z:\My Songs\Workflows\github-cli-auth-and-network-recovery.md` before changing credentials or declaring GitHub unavailable. In particular, distinguish the restricted command sandbox's dead proxy from a genuinely invalid credential by performing one narrowly approved unsandboxed `gh auth status --json hosts` check that cannot reveal the token.

## Codex app lifecycle safety

- Apply this rule to every chat, task, scheduled job and automation in this project.
- Never close, restart, terminate or force-stop ChatGPT, Codex, `codex-code-mode-host`, `codex-computer-use-swift`, or a broad process-name set from an agent-controlled command, script, watcher or delayed background process. Never create or launch a helper that will do so later.
- When maintenance genuinely requires every Codex process to be closed, finish and checkpoint all affected work, prepare a non-running offline procedure, and ask Kieran to close and restart the app manually. An approval to prepare, inspect or migrate data is not permission to terminate the app or interrupt other tasks.
- Do not automatically resume multiple tasks after an app restart. Let Kieran choose which interrupted tasks to reopen unless Kieran explicitly requests the exact resume set after the restart.

## Canonical video players in EPK heroes

- Standing instruction from Kieran, 8 October 2026: whenever a song or release EPK has a canonical video, a playable version of that exact video must occupy part or all of the hero image area at the top of the EPK. Apply this to existing and future pages, chats, tasks, scheduled jobs and automations. Audit existing pages at their next relevant review and include this check in weekly release/EPK sync.
- Resolve the canonical video from current release records, approval and provenance. Preserve its identity, aspect ratio and meaningful picture; do not substitute a trailer, Short, similar video or silent decorative background. If the canonical video is local-only, unpublished, embargoed or not cleared for embedding, prepare the exact candidate and record the publication/availability gate rather than exposing it. Keep the official cover available elsewhere on the page when the player replaces it in the hero.
- Make the real player prominent and usable on desktop, tablet and phone, with visible controls, an accessible name, keyboard operation and fullscreen where supported. A thumbnail, external watch link, hidden player or player below the main hero does not satisfy this rule. Preserve readable hero text, artwork identity, existing EPK content and playback; avoid autoplay with sound. Apply the current motion, clipping and accessibility rules to any surrounding effects.
- Actively work towards applicable Google video enhancements using the current official guidance and `Z:\My Songs\Workflows\epk-video-metadata.md`. Reconcile the actual hero player with canonical video metadata, verify crawler discovery and thumbnail access, and validate the rendered result. A prominent hero player is a project presentation requirement; it does not alone establish Google's watch-page classification, video indexing or rich-result eligibility. If the EPK remains primarily a press kit, record that limit and assess a separate video-focused watch page without changing the EPK's canonical URL or purpose merely to claim eligibility.
- Use the metadata plugin's existing canonical video fields; populate verified required and applicable recommended facts for the same visible video. Add fields to the shared plugin only when an evidenced applicable requirement cannot be represented, with validation, documentation and its matching published release. Keep unknown facts explicit and preserve the exact page/package approval gates. Record hero placement, playback, metadata, crawler access, Google validation and observed search results separately in the release records and EPK preview register.

## EPK thumbnails and playable link previews

- A thumbnail and a play button in search results and social-link previews are an ongoing project goal for every existing and future EPK. Apply `Z:\My Songs\Workflows\epk-video-metadata.md` whenever creating, updating, auditing or promoting an EPK.
- Preserve accurate artwork metadata on every EPK. Where a verified public video is available, maintain its canonical reference, video thumbnail and supported playable-preview metadata, and verify the public player. Do not populate video fields for an unavailable, unverified or mismatched video.
- Maintain `Z:\My Songs\EPK Preview Goals\epk-preview-goals.csv` as the per-page evidence and gap register. Reconcile it with the current catalogue when EPKs or public videos change. Track thumbnail access, video availability, embedding, required video facts, player playback and observed Google/social rendering separately; metadata validity is not proof of a displayed result.
- At each relevant EPK or search/social review, progress the applicable gaps: verify newly available videos and thumbnails, investigate platform requirements and supported cache refreshes, and assess video-focused watch-page presentation where appropriate. Preserve the EPK's purpose and existing content; stage material content or publication changes under the normal approval rules.
- Google and sharing services control their results and cards. Record actual observed thumbnail/play-button outcomes by service and date, or explicitly mark them unverified, unavailable or unsupported. Do not promise universal display, invent dates or handles, or create misleading video markup to meet the goal.

## Public README and project metadata

- Apply this rule whenever creating or updating a project README, package description, repository summary, manifest metadata, release listing or similar adopter-facing project material.
- Treat README files and project metadata as accurate marketing and onboarding material for people who may use the project in their own code. Lead with what the project does, why it is useful, how to install or integrate it, supported environments, current requirements and the shortest relevant path to success. Keep claims proportionate and verifiable.
- Do not copy the project's full internal `## Potential problems` history into public-facing documentation. Include only a problem, limitation or workaround that is currently unresolved or remains directly relevant to a prospective user's installation, compatibility, integration, security, data safety or expected behaviour. Exclude issues that are specific to Kieran's machine, local sandbox, credentials, filesystem layout or one-off working environment unless the same condition is a documented requirement of the released project.
- Remove or omit problems that have been definitively fixed from current README and metadata unless users of an older released version still need a concise migration or compatibility note. Preserve the complete evidence and recovery history in internal reports, workflows, skills, changelogs or maintainer documentation as appropriate; filtering adopter-facing copy must not erase the underlying record.
- Before publishing or packaging, review every README troubleshooting note and metadata warning for current adopter relevance. Rewrite retained items as concise, actionable user guidance rather than a chronological incident log, and verify that README and metadata still present the project clearly without concealing any material current limitation.

## Blog images must include actual artwork

- Standing instruction from Kieran, 11 October 2026: every new blog article needs fresh, article-specific artwork with a meaningful visual subject and composition. Its featured image must be pictorial artwork, such as an illustration, photograph, painting or collage; a text card, table, dashboard or rendered summary alone does not satisfy this requirement.
- Keep useful rendered summaries, diagrams, screenshots and demonstrations as explanatory body media when they help readers. Add actual artwork alongside them. Relevant restrained typography is allowed when imagery is primary. Use the image-generation skill for appropriate new raster illustration requests rather than substituting text rendered into HTML or an image.
- Apply through the project blog writer skill and ordinary blog publishing workflow. Preserve fresh-media, public-use, accessibility, evidence and publication safeguards. Keep actual publicity facts and original links in readable article text; artwork illustrates the subject and must not fabricate an event or endorsement.

## Project blog documentation and publication

- Apply this rule to every chat, task, scheduled job and automation in this project.
- Use `Z:\My Songs\skill-source\project-blog-writer\SKILL.md` whenever a passing subject is drafted, updated, published or verified as an ordinary project blog article; it carries the mandatory in-body rich-media and screen-recording requirements.
- Before declaring any task complete, apply and record a blogworthiness assessment to the activity and its outcome, whether or not the task involved a workflow improvement. Make the pass or fail conclusion and its short evidence-based reason visible in the task's retained report or final handoff.
- Maintain the project-wide blogworthiness test as a living rubric. An activity passes when there is enough verified, public-safe substance for a coherent article and it has at least one clear reader-interest angle: a meaningful creative, musical, technical, research, campaign or wider project advance; an experiment, result, discovery, decision or comparison; a useful process, lesson, fix, failure or recovery; an illuminating behind-the-scenes activity or artefact; a notable milestone or public development; or another specific story that would help, interest or engage readers of Kieran's blog. A process or workflow improvement is only one possible reason to pass and is never required.
- Fail the test when the activity is only trivial administration, substantially duplicates an existing post, lacks enough verified substance for a useful article, has no clear reader-interest angle, or cannot be made public-safe without removing the point of the story. Do not fail something merely because the advance is modest, unusual, unfinished or unrelated to workflow design; an honest work-in-progress, experiment or interesting activity can still be blogworthy when its status is labelled accurately.
- Preserve the detailed evidence for every passing subject in the relevant local report, workflow or skill, then write and publish a WordPress article about it. Combine closely related progress into one coherent entry when that serves readers better, but do not use bundling to indefinitely postpone a subject that already passes the test. Review and improve the rubric when completed posts or missed subjects reveal a repeatable false positive, false negative or useful new reader-interest category.
- Treat the local project record as the detailed evidence and the blog post as a selective public account. Keep every factual claim supportable and current, distinguish verified outcomes from interpretation, proposals and unknowns, explain the practical interest of the work in plain language, and use `Z:\My Songs\skill-source\speak-like-kieran\SKILL.md` for Kieran's natural public voice without inventing feelings, motives or personal recollections.
- This rule is standing authorisation to draft and publish routine qualifying posts to Kieran's WordPress blog without seeking separate confirmation for each post, provided the subject and exact content pass all existing project safeguards. It does not authorise disclosure of private contact or account information, credentials, security-sensitive details, confidential correspondence, controlled rights or permission records, embargoed or deliberately unreleased material, unnecessary third-party personal data, or uncertain claims. Any material rights, privacy, safety, attribution, consent, disclosure or destination-policy uncertainty blocks only the affected post or passage and still requires narrow clarification or approval.
- Before publishing, inspect the current public blog, recent posts, categories, tags and the candidate's local evidence; prevent duplicate or substantially overlapping entries; and choose a truthful title, excerpt, slug, category, tags, date and any media or links. Use the browser-native WordPress workflow, apply form-field readback and committing-action checks, and preserve the site's existing structure and presentation unless a separate change is authorised.
- After publishing, verify the independently loaded signed-out public post, its title, URL, date, body, links, media, category and responsive readability. Record the WordPress post identity, public URL, publication time in Europe/London, source evidence and verification result in the relevant local report or publishing ledger. A saved draft, editor acknowledgement or successful request proves persistence or transmission only; do not call the post published until the public URL is visibly verified. If publication cannot be completed safely, retain a clearly labelled draft and record the smallest specific blocker without holding unrelated project work.

## Project-wide software tool provisioning

- Apply this rule to every chat, task, scheduled job and automation in this project.
- Before obtaining a tool, check whether a suitable version is already available in `Z:\My Songs\Tools` or elsewhere in the project and reuse it when it satisfies the task's requirements.
- When required software is not available, download it from an authoritative source and install or extract a project-wide, self-contained version under `Z:\My Songs\Tools\<tool-name>\<version>\`. Prefer a pinned portable distribution or project-local environment over a machine-wide, user-wide, release-specific or temporary installation. Do not silently substitute an unverified third-party build.
- Record the tool name, exact version, authoritative source URL, download or installation date, licence or redistribution constraints, checksum or signature when available, installed path, exact invocation and a successful version or smoke-test result in `Z:\My Songs\Tools\README.md` or a tool-local manifest. Do not record secrets.
- Future jobs must inspect and use the verified project-wide copy before attempting another download or relying on an ephemeral environment. Upgrade or replace it only deliberately; record the change and retain any older version still needed to reproduce existing work.
- Follow applicable approval, network, licence and security requirements when downloading or executing software. If an authoritative package cannot be obtained, its integrity or permitted use cannot be established, or a project-local installation is technically impossible, do not install an unsafe substitute; report the exact blocker.

## Browser workflow only for web tasks

- Apply this rule to every chat, task, scheduled job and automation in this project.
- For websites and browser-based forms, use the browser-native workflow and its browser-tab controls. Do not use native Windows computer-control, desktop-app automation, screen-coordinate control or any workflow that takes over Kieran's PC merely to operate a website.
- If the browser-native workflow cannot complete a required web action, stop and report the specific browser limitation. Do not silently fall back to native PC control.
- Use native PC or desktop-app control only when Kieran explicitly requests that method for the specific action.
- For authorised website uploads, default to the browser-native file chooser. In the controlled in-app browser, arm `tab.playwright.waitForEvent('filechooser')`, click the site's upload/select-files control, then call the returned chooser's `setFiles()` with the exact absolute local paths. Verify each completed upload in the site's media/library view, including filename and resulting URL or attachment identity; selection alone is not completion. Check `isMultiple()` before a batch. Do not switch to native Windows control because a file input is hidden or because a locator lacks a `setInputFiles` method.

## Close completed browser tabs

Standing instruction from Kieran, 13 October 2026: close browser tabs and windows as soon as their work is finished, preventing completed browser jobs from accumulating unused resources. Apply to every My Songs chat, agent, campaign and recurring job. Follow `Workflows/browser-tab-hygiene.md` through the supported Browser controls.

- Reuse a suitable existing tab before creating another. Track tab ID, purpose and ownership; a tab being hidden or inactive does not prove that it is safe to close.
- After retaining evidence and completing the required verification, close tabs created by the job and borrowed tabs explicitly released by their owner. Close completed preview, diagnostic and read-only verification tabs promptly, not at the next scheduled run.
- Close job-created browser windows after their last required tab is finished, using a documented browser window control when available. If this connection supports tab closure only, close eligible tabs and record any remaining window as a precise limitation; never terminate a browser, Codex or a helper process to simulate window cleanup.
- Retain a tab across turns only for a named next action or an intentional user deliverable. Use the documented handoff or deliverable mark for that specific tab; never mark the entire inventory merely to preserve a connection. Review retained tabs again on the next turn and close them when their purpose ends.
- Preserve active work, unsaved forms, uploads, other owners' tabs and any tab intentionally handed to Kieran. Checkpoint and obtain the applicable authority before discarding unsaved state. Record a retained tab's owner, reason and next action privately.
- Verify closure with a fresh tab list. A close request, timeout, queued panel request or another chat's working connection is not proof of closure or restoration here. Report inaccessible tabs as precise cleanup holds; do not keep opening replacements indefinitely.
- Before troubleshooting, read the known signatures and failed remedies in `Workflows/codex-browser-runtime-recovery.md`; preserve their evidence dates and verification limits. Use bounded, documented connection recovery. Do not stop app-managed helpers as a generic restart: that can leave a closed transport without a replacement. Never terminate or restart Codex/ChatGPT, bypass browser controls, clear sign-ins or weaken permissions. A full-tab reset needs explicit authority and a safe checkpoint; a fresh-chat recovery needs its existing approval.
- Complete cleanup after each finished browser phase and before handing the job back. Record verified closures and each retained tab or window's owner, reason and next action privately. A saved rule alone does not prove that existing windows were closed or that already-running chats adopted it.
- An empty tool inventory establishes only that the selected connection reports no accessible tabs. If Kieran or the visible app shows browser tabs, record a session/window-scope discrepancy and verify the correct documented browser selection; do not label the chat or whole app clean from that empty result. Never inspect profiles/session stores or use private app internals to repair the mismatch. Distinguish tabs the agent successfully closed and independently verified absent from tabs that disappeared through Kieran or another owner; record manual closures separately.
- Keep tab hygiene separate from diagnosis. A high tab count is not evidence of a memory leak, credit-related failure or missing temporary path. Test the previously failing operation before calling a repair complete.

## Form-field write verification

- Apply this rule to every browser or app form completed in this project, including text, email, URL, number, date, search, rich-text and upload-associated fields.
- Before changing a field, identify the exact control and read its current value. When entering a complete value, use one replace operation rather than appending, pasting or typing into an uncleared field.
- After every fill, paste, type, autofill selection or observable manual handoff, read the live value back from that same control and compare it with the exact intended value. Check the whole string, not merely visible fragments or the absence of a validation warning; include length, leading or trailing whitespace, duplicated text, truncation and any site-applied transformation. Recheck all material fields immediately before any submit, send, upload, save, next-step or other committing action because reactive forms can clear, restore or remount controls.
- If the value does not match, do not paste again and do not commit the form. Select all or clear the field, perform one replacement, then read and compare it again. If the field remains unstable or the user manually corrects it, stop automating that field, ask the user to confirm its final value when it cannot be read safely, and do not touch it again before submission.
- Do not expose passwords, tokens, private contact details or other sensitive values in logs or diagnostic output. Verify sensitive fields in place using an appropriate non-disclosing comparison where exact readable inspection is unavailable.
- In the controlled in-app browser, protected input values can be intentionally suppressed from DOM snapshots and evaluation results even when the rendered field is correctly populated. This applies to hidden inputs and to inputs whose `type`, `autocomplete`, `id`, `name`, `placeholder`, `aria-label` or `title` identifies a username, email, one-time code, password/passcode, OTP/2FA/MFA, phone, mobile or telephone value. Never treat an empty protected programmatic readback by itself as evidence that the visible field is empty or unstable.
- For those protected fields, perform one exact replace operation, inspect the rendered control visually when policy permits, and let the site's own validation or next-step response determine whether the syntax is accepted. Do not perform a select-all/type/paste recovery solely because a protected readback is blank; that can duplicate a value which is already present. Passwords, authentication codes, payment data and any other user-only secrets remain subject to their stricter handling rules and must not be exposed or inferred.

## Outreach contact style

- Standing instruction from Kieran, 11 October 2026: whenever we have missed an outreach reply deadline or taken longer than expected to respond, apologise briefly and explicitly ask whether it is too late to proceed. Apply to confirmations, manuscripts, assets and follow-ups. Do not invent excuses, assume an expired invitation remains open, backdate a reply or infer consent from silence. Preserve exact send/submission approval and resolution-evidence requirements.

- Apply this rule project-wide whenever writing an outreach contact, including emails, contact-form messages, pitches, direct messages and follow-ups.
- Write in an informal, humble and conversational style. Sound like Kieran speaking naturally to another person: warm, concise and unassuming, without stiff corporate language, grandiose claims, pressure or exaggerated familiarity.
- Adapt the wording to the recipient and any mandatory format or policy while preserving factual accuracy, appropriate boundaries and the other project-wide outreach safeguards.

## Venue-specific blockers never become campaign-wide blockers

- Apply this rule to every music-marketing, outreach, submission, playlist, press, radio, community and social campaign in this project, including scheduled jobs and automations.
- A requirement, restriction, uncertainty or failure belonging to one venue, route, form, recipient or platform blocks only that specific action. Never generalise it into a campaign-wide hold unless the same condition is independently verified to apply to every remaining route.
- Continue all other safe and eligible campaign work. Record the affected venue, exact requirement, evidence source and smallest dependency needed to unblock it.
- Ask Kieran for information only when a current, specific action genuinely requires it. Do not request provenance, AI-production details, source URLs, rights explanations, clean edits, account changes or other disclosures speculatively because a different or hypothetical venue might ask later.
- If several venues share a requirement, classify and hold those routes individually or as an explicitly evidenced group; do not describe the release or whole campaign as blocked.

## Outreach venue working memory

- Apply this rule project-wide to every marketing or outreach venue, including publications, journalists, reviewers, radio stations, presenters, playlist curators, promoters, communities, support teams and other contacts.
- Create or update one separate venue-specific Markdown working-memory file under `Z:\My Songs\Outreach Venue Memory\` before the first committing contact action. Use a stable venue slug and the same canonical file across releases and campaigns; do not create disconnected release-specific venue memories. A prepared first-contact record must say clearly that contact has not yet been transmitted. After the action, update it from visible evidence with the exact transmitted, receipted, failed or held state.
- Keep the canonical venue registry, audit reports, templates and reusable venue-memory skills or workflows inside `Z:\My Songs`. The canonical registry is `Z:\My Songs\Outreach Venue Memory\venue-registry.csv`; the canonical audit workflow is `Z:\My Songs\skill-source\outreach-venue-memory-audit\SKILL.md`. External agent memory may help locate prior evidence but must not be the authoritative database or workflow for this project.
- Before drafting or making every contact, including a first contact, search for the venue by canonical name, route, domain and known people; read any existing working memory; and reconcile it with relevant release ledgers, sent-message evidence, replies and delivery-status notices. Immediately before committing, recheck the venue memory for duplicate-contact, cooldown, preference, policy and active-conversation constraints. After each new contact, reply, outcome or material policy discovery, append the dated evidence and update the current state without erasing the prior chronology.
- When an already-contacted venue without a canonical working-memory file is next encountered, backfill one from the available evidence before drafting or taking further action. Do not delay current work for an exhaustive portfolio-wide backfill unless that audit is specifically requested.
- Record the venue's canonical name and verified routes; relevant people and roles; dated contact and reply history; releases and materials discussed; stated preferences, requirements, feedback and boundaries; delivery or outcome evidence; follow-up promises, limits and next eligible contact date; unresolved questions; and links or local paths to supporting evidence. Clearly distinguish verified facts, venue statements, Kieran's decisions, interpretation, stale observations and unknowns.
- Keep these files private working records. Include only the personal or sensitive detail needed for continuity, never store secrets, and do not treat transmission, a reply, an acknowledgement or friendly language as proof of coverage, airplay, placement, endorsement or an ongoing relationship.

## Canonical artist contact profile

- Apply this rule to every outreach, submission and account form in this project.
- Read the private local profile at `Z:\My Songs\artist-contact-profile.json` for Kieran's confirmed reusable contact details. Do not ask Kieran to repeat a value that is present there unless the file is missing, unreadable, the destination requires a genuinely different value, or Kieran says the detail has changed.
- Keep the profile private. Do not publish it, quote it in public reports, or expose its sensitive values in diagnostic output. Verify sensitive fields in place using a non-disclosing equality and length check.

## Venue account and public-profile completeness

- Apply this rule project-wide to every platform, website, forum, community, broadcaster, publication, playlist service, social network, creator channel and other venue where this project has an account or makes a public or private contribution.
- Before the first engagement at a venue, and whenever an existing venue is next used, audit the current account and public profile through the venue's supported browser interface. Confirm that the profile is publicly viewable and discoverable wherever the venue offers a suitable public-profile or visibility control, then check every available field and setting that can truthfully improve identification, credibility, discoverability or continuity, including display name, username, artist or real name, biography/about text, website, social links, YouTube, relevant EPK or release page, profile image, cover image, location at the least-specific useful level, occupation or role, interests, signature, pronouns when Kieran has supplied them, and any venue-specific artist, music or portfolio fields. Record fields that are unavailable, prohibited, inapplicable, private-only or deliberately withheld; never invent filler merely to make a form look complete.
- Use `Z:\My Songs\artist-contact-profile.json` as the private project-wide source of confirmed reusable identity, contact and public-profile facts. Use the canonical artist media pack for profile or cover images. Before asking Kieran for any reusable fact, search the global profile and relevant project records. If a material field is genuinely unknown, ask one concise question, then save Kieran's confirmed reusable answer to the global profile with its confirmation date and intended scope so future jobs do not ask again. Never store passwords, authentication codes, payment data or other secrets in that profile.
- Use the personal portfolio and official YouTube channel as the normal external links on general-purpose accounts when the venue permits them. Add a release EPK, song or release-specific YouTube link only when the account or venue is substantially focused on that release or its specific subject, the link is culturally appropriate, and the destination's self-promotion and signature rules permit it. Do not turn a general account into a single-release advert, overload a profile with duplicate links, or add a promotional signature where signatures or self-links are discouraged.
- Write biographies and signatures in Kieran's natural, concise voice using `Z:\My Songs\skill-source\speak-like-kieran\SKILL.md`. Match the venue's ordinary length, formality and purpose; make the artist/creator identity clear without grandiose claims, fabricated affiliations, false expertise or unsupported social proof. A signature should add compact identity and navigation value, not repeat the post body or function as an unsolicited pitch.
- Check the venue's current profile, signature, self-promotion, link, identity and AI-assistance rules before proposing changes. Respect field character limits, link limits, naming policies, real-name requirements and community-specific restrictions. Do not publish private contact details, rights records or sensitive information merely because a field exists, and do not answer an optional demographic or personal field unless Kieran has supplied it and its public use is appropriate.
- Maintain one deduplicated, project-wide venue-account audit ledger inside `Z:\My Songs` with the venue, canonical account and settings URLs, account identity, public/private visibility state, current profile image and source asset, each relevant field and its verified state, rule sources, proposed or applied values, last checked time, verification evidence and any remaining unknowns. Treat platform-wide accounts as one account even when contributions occur in many communities, while recording material community-specific profile or flair differences separately.
- Stage the exact proposed values and destinations before any public save, upload or visibility change and obtain any action-time approval required by the current workflow. Treat making a profile public as a privacy and discoverability change: show Kieran the affected venue, identity and exposed fields immediately before committing it. Do not expose fields that should remain private merely to make the profile public. For every edited field, use one replacement, read the complete live value back, recheck all material fields immediately before saving, and stop if the form is unstable. After saving, verify the persisted settings and the signed-out or public-facing profile; where a profile image or signature should appear beside contributions, also re-open a representative contribution and verify it there. A save receipt alone is not proof of public persistence.
- Re-audit profile completeness after a venue adds fields, changes rules, rejects or strips a link, resets an image, or gives moderator feedback. Every engagement workflow must consider whether the venue profile needs completing before the contribution and must update the account ledger after any profile, signature or identity change.

## Canonical project-wide artist media pack

- Apply this rule to every chat, task, scheduled job and automation in `Z:\My Songs` that needs an artist photograph, portrait, headshot, press image, profile image, EPK image or submission image.
- Before asking Kieran for a photograph or reporting that none is available, inspect `Z:\My Songs\Artist Media Pack\README.md`, `registry\asset-registry.csv`, `registry\asset-registry.json`, `registry\intake-slots.csv`, and the relevant files under `originals\` and `crops\`.
- Treat `Z:\My Songs\Artist Media Pack` as the canonical release-independent artist-photo library. Use registry asset IDs, dimensions, selection tier, suggested contexts, rights status and notes to choose candidates. Prefer an existing suitable crop over creating a duplicate derivative.
- For ordinary general press and EPK use, begin with the current `Recommended first choices` in the pack README, then check release-specific visual fit and the destination's aspect-ratio, resolution, recency and content requirements. Do not call a low-resolution image high-resolution or ignore privacy/visible-watermark notes.
- The standing local rights record is `Z:\My Songs\Artist Media Pack\registry\rights-statement.md`. Keep detailed provenance private and do not expose private locations or account data. Use the recorded default credit unless a specific asset note requires otherwise.
- When Kieran supplies photographs, compare SHA-256 hashes against the originals before copying. Preserve byte-identical matches under their existing asset IDs; add only genuinely missing originals with the next stable ID, update the reproducible builder and all registries, and verify any new dated package. Never overwrite an older dated package.
- If a task needs Kieran to choose between suitable existing images, show the strongest context-matched candidate first and ask one narrow approval question. Do not ask him to locate or reattach files already present in the canonical pack.

## Appointments and scheduling

- Apply this rule to every chat and task in this project.
- Never make, book, confirm, or reschedule an appointment to start before 2:00 p.m. UK local time (`Europe/London`, observing GMT or BST as applicable).
- Never make, book, confirm, or reschedule an appointment with less than seven full days (168 hours) of notice.
- Both constraints must be satisfied. If no compliant time is available, do not make the appointment; report the constraint instead.

## AI involvement and disclosure

- Apply this rule to every chat and task in this project, including public copy, pitches, EPKs, posts, submissions, reports, and scheduled workflows.
- Do not volunteer, append, or attach an AI disclosure unless the recipient's published policy, form, or direct request explicitly requires one.
- When disclosure is explicitly required, acknowledge AI involvement truthfully and use only the detail the recipient requires. Do not publish the exact human/AI production split unless that level of detail is specifically required.
- Keep accurate private provenance evidence sufficient to substantiate the full process to Kieran at will and to answer a legitimate verification request.
- Answer direct or mandatory provenance questions fully and accurately. Never use minimal wording to evade a more specific required question.
- Treat an explicit recipient ban on applicable AI involvement as a stop rule; do not submit or post there.

## Song permissions and rights confidentiality

- Apply this rule to every chat, task, scheduled job and automation in this project, across EPKs, websites, social media, press materials, submissions, public reports and every other public channel.
- Treat all non-public details about a song's permissions, rights, ownership, licences, clearances, samples, agreements, approvals, copyright or publishing splits, and supporting evidence as private by default.
- Never publicly publish or expose any such detail unless Kieran has explicitly authorised the exact information for the specific public destination. General permission to market, submit, update an EPK or publish other song material does not authorise disclosure of permissions or rights details.
- Keep complete rights and permission records privately. If a public policy, form or recipient requires disclosure, stage the exact wording and destination and obtain explicit action-time authorisation before publishing it; if that authorisation is absent, do not publish or submit the disclosure.

## Standing broadcast clearance

- Apply this rule project-wide to promotional outreach and music submissions for Kieran's released songs.
- Kieran gives standing approval to grant a broadcaster, radio station or programme a non-exclusive, no-fee permission to play the submitted recording in its ordinary broadcast or online-radio service and to identify the artist, song and official public links in connection with that play.
- Use the narrowest accurate wording required by the destination. This standing approval does not authorise ownership transfer, exclusivity, sublicensing, commercial synchronisation, downloads, remixing, adaptation, permanent hosting beyond ordinary programme/archive playback, paid terms, warranties, indemnities, collection-society declarations, disclosure of private rights records, or permission covering any song or recording that Kieran has not placed in scope.
- Before granting clearance, verify the exact released recording, destination and published terms. Stop and obtain separate approval if the destination asks for anything beyond the narrow permission above, if the terms are missing or ambiguous, or if the available local rights evidence raises a conflict or uncertainty.
- Record the exact clearance wording, recipient, song, route, timestamp and visible receipt in the release-local outreach ledger. A send or submission receipt proves transmission only, not delivery, acceptance, broadcast or rights interpretation by the recipient.

## Freepik usage rights standing confirmation

- Apply this rule project-wide to every image or derivative in `Z:\My Songs` that Kieran identifies as made with or sourced from Freepik.
- Kieran directly confirmed on 12 September 2026 that he always has the right to use the Freepik images he uses in this project. Treat this as Kieran's standing rights confirmation for ordinary internal campaign work and truthful statements that he has usage rights.
- This confirmation does not independently establish a particular plan tier, licence version/date, asset ID, generative-feature term, exclusivity, copyright ownership, sublicensing right or third-party destination warranty. When a recipient asks for any of those narrower facts or documentary proof, verify the applicable Freepik account, receipt, asset or licence record and stage the exact answer.
- Keep supporting account and licence evidence private. Do not publish the plan, receipt, asset history, prompts or other non-public rights details without Kieran's explicit destination-specific authorisation.

## Public lyrics formatting

- Apply this rule to every public, submitted, displayed or plain-text lyric version in this project, including EPKs, websites, video descriptions, captions, distributor lyric fields, press materials and lyric sheets.
- Use the explicit, uncensored performed lyrics by default. Do not substitute euphemisms, masking characters, omissions or a clean rewrite merely because the destination is public.
- Create or use a clean lyric version when the destination specifically requires clean wording, or as an optional clearly identified social-media or broadcast-friendly promotional derivative. A clean version is not required merely because content is being posted on social media, and it must never replace or silently alter the canonical explicit lyrics.
- Whenever an explicit lyric, audio or video version is published, submitted or uploaded, mark it as explicit in every applicable metadata field or content control offered by the destination. If no formal field exists and the explicit nature would otherwise be unclear, include a concise visible content notice.
- Omit structural section labels such as `[Intro]`, `[Verse]`, `[Chorus]`, `[Bridge]`, `[Spoken Word]` and `[Outro]` from lyrics intended for reading, publication, submission or display.
- Keep section labels only in LRC or other cue-oriented timing files when they are useful for synchronisation or internal structure. Do not copy those labels from a cue file into the public lyric text.
- Before publishing or submitting lyrics, compare the destination text with the canonical performed words after removing cue-only section labels; preserve the performed wording, order and intentional line breaks.

## Clean-edit audio workflow

- Apply this rule to every clean, radio-safe, broadcast-safe or profanity-edited audio decision in this project. The canonical performed lyrics are authoritative for profanity status. If they contain no profanity, treat the unchanged recording as profanity-clean and do not manufacture a clean edit. Keep profanity status separate from an intentional platform `Explicit` or content-warning designation: a song may remain correctly marked explicit because of drug use, violence, dangerous behaviour, sexual material, crisis or other mature lyrical subject matter even when it contains no rude words.
- If the canonical performed lyrics do contain a rude word or expletive and a clean derivative is required, preserve the canonical explicit master byte-for-byte and create a separately named derivative that clearly includes `Clean Edit`; never overwrite, relabel or silently substitute the explicit version.
- For each confirmed rude word or expletive, cross-fade from the canonical master to a verified, time-aligned karaoke or everything-but-lead-vocal stem before the complete audible word, hold the karaoke stem through the word and any audible vocal tail, then cross-fade smoothly back to the master. Use short, individually adjusted equal-power or comparably smooth fades so consonants, breaths, reverb and neighbouring permitted words are not clipped and no abrupt mute or level dip is introduced.
- Use only a karaoke stem derived from the same final programme. Before editing, compare duration, sample rate, channel layout and alignment with the canonical master; prefer the best-aligned available version and record the evidence. Do not time-stretch a materially mismatched stem or use an accompaniment containing audible replacement vocals or unrelated artefacts. If the word timing or stem alignment is ambiguous, stop and produce an exact edit brief for attended listening instead of guessing.
- Preserve the programme duration and, where technically possible, the master format, channel layout and level outside the edit windows. Record the source paths and hashes, selected stem/version, exact edit windows and fade lengths, tool or command used, output path and output hash in a private release-local edit record.
- Verify any final derivative by inspecting and listening across every edit boundary and through the full programme. Require that no targeted expletive remains audible, neighbouring words remain intact, transitions are smooth, the music bed remains continuous, duration and channel layout match the master within container tolerance, and metadata identifies the derivative accurately. A waveform or automated scan supports but never replaces listening verification.
- Keep public canonical lyrics uncensored. Create a separately labelled clean lyric derivative only when the canonical lyrics actually contain profanity and a destination specifically requires it. A profanity-clean recording may still require an accurate content note and may remain unsuitable for a recipient whose policy excludes its themes; never describe `profanity-clean` as `family-friendly` or `content-safe` without separately checking those requirements.

## YouTube age restriction for expletives

- Apply this rule to every future YouTube upload in this project, including full videos, official audio, lyric videos, visualisers, Shorts and scheduled assets.
- If the final uploaded audio contains any spoken or sung expletive, explicitly set the YouTube audience restriction to `Yes, restrict my video to viewers over 18` before publishing or scheduling. An explicit notice in the title or description does not replace this control.
- Treat a clean derivative as exempt only after checking the exact final audio and corresponding performed lyric against the canonical explicit version and verifying that no expletive remains.
- Re-read the persisted age-restriction state immediately before the final publish or schedule action and verify the resulting public asset is age-gated. Record the control state in the release's publishing evidence.
- Do not appeal or remove a restriction that is consistent with this rule merely to increase reach. If YouTube applies a different policy category or restriction for another reason, record the exact platform state separately and ask Kieran before appealing.

## Default video frame rate

- Apply this rule project-wide whenever a video is generated, edited, rendered, exported or otherwise finalised, including full-length videos, visualisers, lyric videos, trailers, Shorts, Reels, TikToks, stories, teasers, drafts, and scheduled or automated outputs.
- Generate and deliver the exact final encoded video at constant 60 frames per second by default. Request 60 fps from the source generator when its verified schema supports it, and keep 60 fps through editing and export rather than reducing it later in the workflow.
- Use a different frame rate only when the destination explicitly requires it, an authoritative source must be preserved at its native rate, or the generator or production chain cannot reliably produce 60 fps. Record the exact reason, source rate, output rate, and any conversion or interpolation used. Do not describe duplicated or interpolated frames as native 60 fps motion, and do not force conversion when it introduces visible artefacts, timing drift, or audio-sync errors.
- Before approval, upload, scheduling or publication, probe the exact final encoded deliverable and verify its reported average and nominal frame rates, constant-frame-rate behaviour, duration and audio sync. Inspect representative motion and any converted sections for dropped, duplicated or interpolated-frame artefacts; rerender avoidable defects or retain a documented exception.

## Promotional covers for every video and short upload

- Apply this rule project-wide to every new video or short-form upload, scheduling or publication, including full-length videos, official audio, lyric videos, visualisers, trailers, Shorts, Reels, TikToks, stories, teasers and scheduled or automated outputs.
- Before uploading, generate a separate, unique promotional cover image for the exact video. Use the release's current visual-language record and the video's actual subject, mood and campaign purpose. Do not substitute the canonical square cover, a generic batch image, an automatically chosen video frame or an informative opening frame for this separate asset unless Kieran explicitly chooses that treatment.
- Every cover image must clearly include the exact canonical song name and artist name within the image itself. Keep both names readable at native and phone-thumbnail size, with sufficient contrast and placement that survives each destination's crop and interface overlays. A post caption, video title or description does not satisfy this requirement. Verify both names in the selected and persisted cover during thumbnail QA.
- Every distinct video or Short must have a compositionally unique cover: change the scene arrangement, subject framing, camera/viewpoint or meaningful visual hierarchy to suit that exact video's content. A crop, resize, colour variation, text swap or added border on another video's cover does not count as a new composition. Compare the proposed cover with the other covers for that release before use and record the distinguishing composition in its provenance. Platform size/crop derivatives of the same exact video's cover may share its composition; do not assign that composition to a different video or materially revised Short.
- Inspect the cover at native and phone-thumbnail size. Keep its subject, truthful identifying text, contrast and destination-specific crop legible; prepare platform-specific size/crop derivatives where needed. Preserve the original video and cover assets, and record their paths, SHA-256 hashes, generation/source provenance and intended destinations in the release-local manifest or publishing ledger.
- Inspect each destination's current supported cover controls. Upload the image separately when supported. Where only video-frame selection is supported, follow the platform-specific derivative and selection procedure in `Z:\My Songs\SHORT-FORM-PUBLISHING-WORKFLOW.md`; preserve the approved original, audio timing and later frames, and recheck the exact derivative. A platform limitation does not waive preparing the promotional cover. Record an unavailable route or failed application as an outstanding gate instead of silently accepting an automatic thumbnail.
- Before committing, read back the selected cover. After saving or scheduling, independently inspect the exact item's thumbnail on the owned-content or scheduled-content surface, reload once and verify it again. After publication, check the public profile/grid/watch surface where available. Record the platform ID/URL, cover asset/hash, checked time, inspected surface and `thumbnail QA: pass`, `fail` or `unavailable` separately from upload, schedule and publication state.
- The final checklist must require cover generation, application and independent persistence verification for every item and destination. Do not report a publishing task fully complete when that gate is missing, failed or unavailable; retain the exact outstanding task or Kieran's explicit exception. When reviewing existing uploads, record missing covers or evidence and prepare recovery through supported routes without treating this rule as approval to delete, recreate or change public posts.
- Use `Z:\My Songs\Workflows\SHORT-FORM-VIDEO-WORKFLOW.md` for short-form asset acceptance and `Z:\My Songs\SHORT-FORM-PUBLISHING-WORKFLOW.md` for publishing and thumbnail QA. Full-video scheduling also inherits this gate through `Z:\My Songs\Workflows\release-video-social-scheduling.md`. The separate opening-frame requirement below still applies.

## Generated video and short-form starting frames

- Apply this rule to every generated or rendered video in this project, including full-length videos, visualisers, trailers, Shorts, Reels, TikToks, stories, teasers, drafts intended for review, and scheduled or automated outputs.
- The first decoded frame of the final encoded file must be a deliberate, suitable opening image rather than a blank, black, unrendered or transitional frame. It must contain the key truthful identifying information or visual hook that should be understood in a thumbnail for that asset, using the release's current visual guidance and remaining legible at phone-thumbnail size.
- Do not rely on a fade-in, delayed title or caption, platform-generated preview, later frame, or separately uploaded thumbnail to make an unsuitable starting frame acceptable. A separately selected thumbnail may differ from the opening frame, but the opening frame must still satisfy this rule.
- Hold the informative opening composition long enough to survive ordinary decoding, transcoding and preview generation without exposing a blank or incomplete lead-in. Do not damage intentional audio timing or make unsupported claims merely to satisfy the visual requirement.
- Before approval, upload, scheduling or publication, extract and inspect the first decoded frame from the exact final encoded deliverable, not only from a source timeline or intermediate render. Check it at native size and representative phone-thumbnail size for visible imagery, correct text, truthful key information, legibility, safe-area placement and absence of black or blank regions caused by a failed render. Reject and rerender any asset that fails.
- When a platform supports a separately selected thumbnail or cover frame, choose and verify a suitable one as a separate publishing check. The starting-frame requirement applies whether or not that platform thumbnail is selected, supported or honoured. Release-specific workflows inherit this rule and may strengthen but not waive it.

## Short-form text placement and platform-overlay QA

- Apply this rule project-wide to every short-form video, including TikToks, Reels, Shorts, stories, teasers and platform-specific derivatives, whether generated, rendered, edited, approved, uploaded, scheduled or published.
- Keep all authored text, lyrics, titles, captions, logos and essential UI in the horizontal middle and the vertical middle or upper-middle of the video. Leave a deliberate margin above the text for platform toolbars and device cropping. Do not place essential authored text in the lower third, near the side-action rail, or against any edge merely because the unoverlaid master frame has room there.
- Resolve the safe region separately for each destination from the freshest official overlay, safe-zone asset or platform preview available for the exact format, caption length and add-ons. Preserve that dated source and its hash with the asset QA evidence. Do not treat a generic 9:16 template, an old screenshot or another platform's layout as current proof.
- For TikTok, inspect the exact final encoded file beneath TikTok's current official in-feed overlay before approval. Extract frame zero and representative frames for every distinct authored-text state, composite the actual official overlay or safe-zone mask at the video's encoded dimensions, and verify that no text bounding box intersects the top toolbar, right-side controls, caption/identity area, bottom navigation or cropped margin. TikTok states that previews may differ by device, so also recheck the exact uploaded asset in TikTok's platform preview immediately before posting. If that preview is unavailable before upload, retain the asset as technical-QA only; do not claim live-overlay verification.
- Record the source URL and access date, overlay filename and hash, final video hash, inspected frames/text states, measured text bounds, any collision found, and the result. If text overlaps, approaches the exclusion boundary without a defensible margin, or cannot be verified, reposition and rerender it rather than accepting the risk. A clean overlay composite does not waive readability, contrast, lyric accuracy, opening-frame or accessibility checks.

## Loopable short-form video

- Apply this rule project-wide whenever a short-form video is conceived, generated, edited, rendered, approved, uploaded, scheduled or published, including TikToks, Reels, Shorts, stories, teasers and platform-specific derivatives.
- Make short-form videos loop cleanly wherever the material permits, with particular priority for every TikTok upload. Plan the loop during concept and edit decisions rather than treating it as a last-minute transition: where practical, align the ending with the opening in composition, movement, timing and audio so automatic replay feels continuous and intentional.
- The audio must loop cleanly too. For music-led videos, choose the excerpt and seam on compatible beat, bar and musical-phrase boundaries; where practical, beat-match the ending to the opening downbeat and preserve continuous tempo, groove, level, ambience and tonal character across the replay. Never cut a sung or spoken word, lyric line or vocal phrase at the loop seam. Let the line and any necessary vocal tail finish, and ensure the restart neither omits nor doubles syllables. Use only a short, transparent crossfade or other transition when it improves the seam without creating a doubled beat, smeared transient, ghost vocal or timing drift.
- Do not force a seamless loop when it would damage the truthful sequence, musical phrasing, lyric meaning, accessibility, caption legibility, necessary call to action, opening-frame requirement or overall creative quality. When a fully seamless loop is unsuitable, prefer the least disruptive end-to-start transition that still encourages a natural replay.
- Before approval, upload, scheduling or publication, inspect and listen to repeated playback of the exact final encoded file across its last-to-first-frame seam. Check for black or duplicate frames, visual jumps, exposure or colour changes, disappearing or displaced elements, clipped motion, dead air, audio clicks, doubled or mistimed beats, abrupt level or ambience changes, and any cut-off word, lyric line, vocal phrase or musical phrase. Reject and rerender avoidable seam defects; if a material exception is retained, record the reason in the asset's production or publishing evidence.
- For TikTok, recheck the exact uploaded asset and its platform preview before posting to ensure that cropping, cover selection, captions, added audio or platform processing has not broken the intended loop. A loop-friendly edit does not remove the requirement for a deliberate, informative first decoded frame or a separately verified platform cover.

## New release media and EPK inclusion

- Apply this rule whenever new release-specific media is generated, rendered, commissioned, edited or otherwise finalised in this project, including artwork, approved artwork variants, photographs, videos, Shorts, Reels, TikToks, visualisers, lyric videos, trailers, teasers, animations and other campaign media.
- Treat adding each final, public-ready media asset to the corresponding release's canonical WordPress EPK as part of the same media workflow, not as an optional later task. The media workflow is not complete until the asset is included on the EPK and verified, or its EPK inclusion is explicitly recorded as pending or blocked with the exact reason.
- Do not add drafts, rejected variants, private masters, editable source files, review-only assets, duplicates, or media whose public use is unverified. Respect embargoes, destination policies, explicit-status requirements, privacy and rights controls. Generation or approval of an asset does not by itself authorise a public upload or WordPress change; when action-time approval is still required, stage the exact EPK addition and request that approval immediately before publication.
- Inspect the rendered live EPK first and merge the media into a release-appropriate location without removing or weakening existing content or functionality. Prefer a verified official public URL or embed for already published media; before uploading a local asset, verify the exact file, identity, hash, dimensions or duration, format, accessibility treatment and intended WordPress destination.
- Give each addition an accurate accessible name and any useful concise context, use responsive presentation, preserve native controls, avoid autoplay, and use an appropriate verified poster or opening frame for video. Do not imply that a teaser, excerpt, preview or Short is the full recording.
- After publication, verify the persisted media signed out at desktop, tablet and mobile widths: confirm correct release identity, successful image loading or playable media, keyboard access, accessible names, no unexpected autoplay, no duplicate or broken presentation and no horizontal overflow. Then reconcile the release-local EPK mirror, generator, manifest, media inventory and report so future work retains the asset.

## Review audio belongs on the normal release EPK

- Apply this rule project-wide whenever audio is prepared for reviewers, press, radio contacts, playlist curators or other marketing recipients.
- Never create a password-protected, hidden, reviewer-only or separate review page. Publish the approved review audio on the song's normal canonical WordPress EPK page and use that EPK URL in outreach.
- Use a verified public-ready derivative such as the canonical 320 kbps MP3; never expose the private WAV master, source stems, credentials, expiring links or private production evidence.
- Present the audio in an accessible native, non-autoplay player with accurate song identity and explicit-content treatment. Do not imply that public availability proves rights clearance, editorial consideration or recipient engagement.
- Stage the exact file, hash, byte size, player placement and EPK payload before publication, obtain any otherwise-required action-time approval, and verify the persisted audio signed out at desktop, tablet and mobile widths.

## Verified publicity, airplay and discussion archives

- Whenever airplay is announced or discovered, actively try to obtain a lawful recording as evidence: prepare capture for a known upcoming broadcast where supported, or promptly check official catch-up/archive sources afterward. Aim to preserve the complete song plus roughly two minutes either side, including presenter context; record any failed attempt or unavailable source rather than silently skipping capture. A scheduled play remains scheduled until actual play is verified.
- Record every verified airplay instance on the song's canonical EPK, with station, programme, broadcast date/time when known and a source link or cleared evidence exhibit. A missing or non-republishable recording does not by itself block a truthful EPK airplay entry supported by other primary evidence. Keep programme audio private unless public reuse is cleared; retain the normal exact-payload approval and signed-out verification gates, and track EPK inclusion as pending until completed.
- Apply this rule project-wide whenever publicity, broadcast airplay or a public or authorised discussion is found that relates to one of Kieran's songs. Follow `Z:\My Songs\Workflows\VERIFIED-COVERAGE-EPK-ARCHIVE.md`.
- Do not treat a pitch, submission, acknowledgement, promise of future play, playlist add without playback evidence, schedule listing, search snippet, third-party claim or transmission receipt as publicity, completed airplay or discussion. Verify the source, song identity, date and relevant content from the original publication, programme, platform or another independent primary record before archiving it as confirmed.
- Preserve a release-local evidence archive for every verified instance. Record the original URL or source identity, outlet or venue, programme or discussion title, relevant people or roles when public, publication or broadcast date and timezone when available, access date, verification method, exact song reference, capture boundaries, file identity and SHA-256 hash. Keep an unedited evidence copy where capture is permitted; never expose private correspondence, account data, review credentials or non-public rights information.
- For verified radio airplay, locate the exact play in the programme whenever a lawful recording or official catch-up source is available. Aim to retain roughly two minutes before the song begins and two minutes after it ends so the DJ's introduction, back-announcement or discussion is included; extend the private evidence window when relevant speech crosses those boundaries. Record programme-relative timestamps and listen through the complete retained segment before labelling it.
- Treat private preservation and public reuse as separate decisions. Kieran's standing permission for a broadcaster to play a submitted recording does not grant permission to republish the broadcaster's programme or the DJ's speech. Publish the contextual recording on the EPK only when its reuse is supported by the source's terms, a suitable licence or permission. Otherwise preserve the full contextual capture privately and use the broadcaster's own public embed or link, or a verified screenshot or concise attributed extract whose public use is permitted. Do not bypass DRM, authentication, paywalls, access controls or expiring-media protections to obtain a capture.
- Treat EPK inclusion as part of the same evidence workflow. Inspect the rendered live EPK first, stage a merge-first addition with an accurate source link, date, context and accessible media treatment, and obtain action-time approval for the exact public payload before any upload or WordPress save. After publication, verify the persisted evidence signed out at desktop, tablet and mobile widths and reconcile the release-local archive, EPK mirror, manifest and report. Until that verification passes, record the exact state as verified and archived, EPK staged, publication blocked or publication pending rather than claiming it is on the EPK.

## WordPress song BPM metadata

- Apply this rule to every WordPress song-page or EPK creation, update, redesign, repair, scheduled job and automation across the project.
- Whenever a song's BPM is known, always specify the verified canonical BPM in that song's dedicated `BPM` field in the WordPress page editor. Do not treat BPM shown only in page content, code, animation settings or local records as a substitute for the editor field. Read the complete field value back before saving and verify that it persisted after the save. If the BPM is unknown, do not invent or infer a value merely to populate the field; record the gap as unknown.
- For DanceMoves EPKs, resolve the runtime BPM from that exact page's saved `_dance_moves_bpm` metadata through the plugin's page configuration. Page-specific effects must consume the plugin-resolved BPM (`DanceMoves.bpm` and the matching `.ks-epk` BPM/source attributes), not embed a separate numeric tempo or override it in clock calls. Treat the plugin's 120 BPM fallback as an explicit unknown-metadata state, never as proof of the song's tempo; withhold beat-synchronised release-specific effects until the page metadata is saved and verified. Keep a regression test for the metadata-to-runtime path before plugin releases.

## WordPress EPK Open Graph and social-sharing metadata

- Apply this rule to every WordPress EPK creation, update, redesign, repair, audit, scheduled job and automation across the project. Treat a missing, generic, stale or conflicting social-sharing package as an incomplete EPK and backfill it when that EPK is next worked on.
- Every EPK must expose one authoritative, release-specific metadata set in the server-rendered document `<head>`, rather than relying on body markup or client-side JavaScript. Include a matching canonical link and, at minimum, `og:title`, `og:type` set to `music.song`, `og:url`, `og:image`, `og:image:alt`, `og:description`, `twitter:card` set to `summary_large_image`, `twitter:title`, `twitter:description`, `twitter:image` and `twitter:image:alt`. Use the verified song title, artist, concise release-specific description and exact canonical EPK URL; do not let a theme or SEO plugin emit a second conflicting set.
- Use a public, absolute HTTPS image URL that resolves signed out to the correct release artwork or an approved release-specific social card. Prefer a purpose-made 1200 x 630 pixel social card when available; otherwise use the strongest verified official artwork without misrepresenting its dimensions or crop. Supply accurate `og:image:type`, `og:image:width` and `og:image:height` values, and keep the image alternative text factual and concise. Never substitute unrelated generic site art merely to fill the field.
- Include `og:audio`, `og:audio:secure_url` and `og:audio:type` only when they can reference an approved, direct, signed-out public audio file for the exact recording. Never expose a private master, WAV, review credential, expiring URL or controlled asset through social metadata. When the canonical duration is verified, add `music:duration` as the whole-track length in integer seconds. Add `music:album` with its following `music:album:disc` and `music:album:track` structured properties only when a verified public album-object URL and canonical one-based disc and track numbers exist. Add `music:musician` only as a verified public artist-profile URL. These Open Graph properties are URL-valued relationships, not fields for plain album or artist names; omit unknown or inapplicable values rather than inventing them.
- Derive the metadata from canonical release records and the current live page, and keep the page title, description, artwork, audio, duration, album context, artist identity, canonical URL and explicit-content presentation mutually consistent. Social metadata must not disclose private rights, permissions, provenance, contact details or embargoed material, and it must not imply that a preview is the full recording.
- Before saving, read back the complete generated metadata and check for duplicate or conflicting canonical, Open Graph and Twitter Card tags. After saving, inspect the signed-out server response and require the tags to be present in `<head>` with the exact persisted values; verify that the canonical, image, audio and referenced object URLs resolve publicly with the expected identity, MIME type and dimensions or duration. Then refresh and inspect the available Facebook and X/Twitter link-preview or scraper tools, recording cache limitations separately. A successful WordPress save or correct source tags alone do not prove that a social network rendered the intended title, artwork and description.

## WordPress EPK lyric and cue timing metadata

- Apply this rule to every WordPress EPK creation, update, redesign, repair, audit, scheduled job and automation across the project.
- Before staging an EPK change, inspect the song's release-local canonical records for an accepted canonical lyric-timing file and an accepted canonical cue-timing file. If either exists, upload it to or reuse its byte-identical attachment in the WordPress Media Library and select that attachment in the EPK page's corresponding `Lyric Timing File` or `Cue Timing File` metadata field. If both exist, both are required. A file URL, page-body reference, local record or completed Media Library upload does not substitute for the dedicated metadata field.
- Use only the file verified as canonical for the exact released recording. Do not upload or select estimated, rejected, low-match, duration-mismatched, superseded, provenance-only, analysis-only or pending-listening-QA files as canonical metadata. If no accepted canonical file exists for a field, leave that field unset rather than guessing; do not clear an existing verified field without separately establishing that it is stale or wrong.
- Before upload or selection, verify the exact file identity, status, format, strict UTF-8 validity, timestamp monotonicity, programme/duration agreement and SHA-256 hash. Reuse an existing byte-identical WordPress attachment where possible; otherwise stage the exact public upload and obtain any required action-time approval before the upload or page save.
- Immediately before saving, read back the complete `Lyric Timing File` and `Cue Timing File` selections together with the other material EPK fields. After saving, verify the persisted attachment IDs, exact public URLs, hashes and page metadata, then confirm signed-out that the EPK's public runtime resolves the selected files for the correct song. Treat an available canonical file that is not both uploaded and selected in its metadata field as an incomplete EPK; record any blocked backfill with the exact reason rather than reporting the EPK complete.

## Public-copy marketing and keyword gate

- Standing instruction from Kieran, 11 October 2026: apply this rule to every current and future project chat, task, scheduled job and automation that drafts, generates, reviews, stages, schedules, publishes or audits public copy. It covers EPKs, other website pages, posts, captions, titles, descriptions, metadata, press materials, pitches and public replies, including text inserted by templates, snippets, scripts and runtime status messages. This is a mandatory pre-publication check, not optional stylistic guidance.
- All promotional public copy must be audience-facing marketing or press copy: give the intended reader a truthful reason to care, understand the song or artist, listen, explore or take the appropriate next action. Use `Z:\My Songs\skill-source\speak-like-kieran\SKILL.md`; keep Kieran's natural voice and the destination's purpose. Marketing copy does not mean hype, sales jargon or unsupported claims. For a genuine non-promotional article or discussion, serve that reader's topic rather than forcing a song promotion.
- Before drafting and again before the committing action, read the campaign's current keyword policy or Keyword analysis and the song's relevant rows in `Z:\My Songs\Keyword Targets\keyword-targets.csv`, following **Song keyword targeting** above. Check adopted versus proposed status, evidence date, audience/search intent and exact platform/surface. Use appropriate adopted phrases naturally; do not stuff keywords, force one into every sentence, claim unverified demand or transfer demand between platforms. Missing or stale evidence must be recorded privately, not invented or used as a reason to publish misleading copy.
- Never put design direction, implementation commentary, mechanical effect descriptions, timing/phase/matcher details, QA language, preservation assurances or build notes into promotional public copy. For example, `Static until playback. Reflection accents appear in selected musical passages.` fails this gate. Keep such information in private build, visual-style and evidence reports. A project-development article may explain technical work only when that work is its intended editorial subject; that exception does not make engineering commentary appropriate song EPK copy.
- Apply the same rule before rendering any campaign artwork, lyric video, visualiser, Short, Canvas, trailer, thumbnail or social asset. Treat every visible title, subtitle, lower third, caption, slate, watermark, accessibility label and generated template string as audience-facing copy. Production labels and placeholders such as effect names, palette or scene directions, transition notes, BPM/beat/phase language, matcher or recording names, QA status and words like `preview`, `test`, `proof` or `candidate` must stay in private project metadata unless that technical subject is explicitly the audience-facing editorial purpose. Before delivery, scheduling or publication, inspect the exact render project/payload for visible strings and inspect representative encoded frames, including the opening, each text or scene change and the ending. A technically successful encode fails this gate if it exposes private production language. Add deterministic assertions for known forbidden build labels where a maintained renderer or project adapter can support them.
- Necessary controls, error messages, accessibility text, factual alternative text, credits, explicit-content notices and required disclosures remain accurate and concise rather than keyword-stuffed promotional prose. These narrow functional exceptions must help the visitor act or understand relevant content; they must not become a channel for explaining the implementation. Preserve canonical lyrics verbatim rather than rewriting them for marketing or keywords.
- Review the complete exact candidate's visible text, accessible names, captions, metadata and all reachable runtime messages, not just its main paragraphs. Record the audience/purpose, keyword-policy evidence or gap, and copy-check result in the existing private report or manifest. Rewrite or remove failing copy before requesting publication approval, scheduling or saving. Repeat the check after generated inserts and fresh-source merges; after publication inspect rendered text and exercised status states. A source-only scan, polished opening paragraph or successful save is not proof that the gate passed.
- When an existing public item is next worked on, audit it against this rule and prepare any required correction without silently widening publication authority. Preserve approval, rights, privacy and destination-policy gates. Do not publish a failing candidate merely because the wording was already live, generated by a tool or present in a canonical template.

## Test EPKs across common mobile widths

- On every EPK creation, repair, publication and recurring audit, follow `Workflows/epk-responsive-layout-qa.md`. Check 320, 375, 390, 414 and 430 CSS pixels, the 760px mobile boundary, and 768/900/1440px tablet/desktop layouts. Add just below/at/above any changed breakpoint. One 390px screenshot is insufficient.
- Verify the actual inner/client width and height after applying a viewport. A requested phone override that still measures 1280px is unavailable evidence, not a mobile pass. Independently read screenshot pixel dimensions; reject miniature-page captures on a wider canvas. Keep browser emulation distinct from physical phone/Safari evidence.
- Inspect artwork and content gutters, hero/title/date/actions, below-fold media, lyrics, facts, downloads, navigation and contact controls. Check rendered text ranges and control rectangles against the viewport and clipping ancestors as well as document scroll width: `overflow:hidden` can conceal a broken layout. Verify declared centering/alignment geometrically; preserve intentional left-aligned copy, full-bleed art and release-specific compositions.
- Collect observations with `Tools/EPK Motion QA/collect_epk_layout.js` through the supported Browser, and run `Tools/EPK Motion QA/audit_epk_layout.py` on the private evidence JSON. Missing checks, missing phone widths, unapplied viewports, clipped text/controls, uneven declared gutters, unloaded artwork and wrong-size/unreviewed screenshots fail the gate. Intentional scroll navigation and offscreen skip links require explicit touch/keyboard review.
- Preview the exact candidate and repeat the same matrix against the persisted anonymous page after an approved save. Preserve source hashes, timing metadata, media, mobile input and existing publication gates. Recheck enlarged text, reduced motion and relevant player/menu open states; close completed owned test tabs and reset temporary viewport overrides. A local candidate pass is not a live repair.

## WordPress EPK merge and preservation

- Apply this rule to every EPK update, redesign, repair, scheduled job and automation across the project.
- Apply the mandatory **Public-copy marketing and keyword gate** above to the complete EPK and every generated or runtime text addition before publication; the EPK-specific rules below supplement that project-wide gate.
- Treat every piece of visitor-facing EPK text as deliberately crafted marketing or press copy. Write and review it using `Z:\My Songs\skill-source\speak-like-kieran\SKILL.md` (the **Write as Kieran** skill), grounded in verified release evidence and shaped for the page's intended audience. Every heading, paragraph, caption, label, instruction and call to action must help present the artist, song, story, release facts, listening experience, press assets, editorial context or a useful visitor action; do not use public copy as a place to document the page itself.
- Never publish design direction, implementation commentary, feature descriptions, QA language or build notes as EPK copy. This includes statements about where the visual language came from, what artwork supplied the design, which controls remain available, whether audio autoplays, how effects or interactions work, animation timing, responsive behaviour, reduced-motion fallbacks, accessibility implementation or other behind-the-scenes decisions. In particular, phrases such as `The native audio controls remain available. Audio never autoplays.` and `This page takes its visual language directly from the supplied release artwork.` belong in private build, visual-style, accessibility or QA records, not on the published page.
- Implement and verify functional, accessibility and safety requirements without narrating them to visitors. Where a visible instruction, content notice, credit, disclosure or control label is genuinely required, write the narrowest useful audience-facing wording in Kieran's voice rather than explaining the design or engineering. Remove or rewrite existing technical or design-description copy whenever an EPK is next created, updated, repaired, audited or republished; do not preserve it merely because the live page is otherwise canonical.
- The canonical WordPress EPK page must always use the song title as its slug, normalised only into WordPress’s lowercase hyphenated URL form. Never give the EPK a numeric, `epk`, `press-kit` or other suffix merely because an attachment or another temporary item claimed that slug first. Before uploading media or creating the page, check the intended title slug and every upload-derived attachment slug for collisions. Move the conflicting attachment or temporary-item slug to a clear descriptive alternative while preserving its direct media URL and bytes, then verify that the unsuffixed song-title slug belongs to the EPK. Do not delete, overwrite or replace a conflicting item without separate authorisation.
- Every EPK must be responsive and provide, at minimum, deliberate layouts for desktop, tablet and mobile viewports. Treat a missing or non-functional layout at any of those three viewport classes as an incomplete EPK or a regression.
- Treat the currently rendered live WordPress EPK as canonical. Before preparing a change, inspect its visible content, links, embeds, players, downloads, calendar actions, contact routes, shared navigation, accessibility behaviour and responsive layout. Local builds, prototypes and snippets are subordinate and may be stale.
- Use a merge-first strategy: preserve all existing facts and working features, then add or alter only the scoped elements required by the task. Do not replace the live page with a visually attractive prototype that omits current information or functionality.
- Prefer scoped additive CSS, progressive enhancement and narrowly inserted semantic markup. Keep the existing WordPress template, release-specific identity and official cover unchanged unless the user explicitly requests their replacement.
- A live item missing from the candidate is a regression unless the user explicitly requested its removal. Maintain a parity checklist for material updates and compare the candidate against the live page before publication.
- Before the final WordPress update, stage the exact merged payload and obtain any required action-time confirmation. After updating, verify the persisted page signed out at desktop, tablet and mobile widths, check keyboard and reduced-motion behaviour, and confirm every retained link, player, download and contact feature. Use a recoverable WordPress revision as the rollback point.
- Reconcile durable local mirrors, generators, reports and manifests to the verified live result so later jobs do not reintroduce stale or incomplete markup.

## EPK release-day listening transition

- Apply this rule to every EPK, release-day update, scheduled job and automation across the project.
- Treat a release as live for this transition only after verifying signed-out public full-length playback for the correct artist, title and release identity at an official destination. The release date arriving, distributor delivery, a platform listing, a pre-save or HyperFollow page, and a partial or preview stream do not by themselves prove that the release is live.
- On release day, if no suitable signed-out public full-length version exists, create a public-ready full-length MP3 derivative from the canonical release audio, without altering the programme. Verify its identity, duration, audio completeness, metadata and explicit-status handling against the canonical release records; never upload the private master or a WAV. Stage the exact file, hash, byte size and intended WordPress destination, then obtain action-time approval before uploading it to the WordPress Media Library. Verify the persisted public URL, MIME type, byte size and complete signed-out playback, then use that verified URL in an accessible native, non-autoplay EPK player. The verified WordPress-hosted playback may serve as the official full-length destination needed to complete this transition.
- Once the release is verified live, remove the complete visitor-facing calendar-add feature from its EPK, including the trigger, Google and Outlook links, downloadable iCal data, reminder copy and now-unused calendar markup or scripts. Do not merely hide an obsolete calendar control with CSS.
- In the same release-day update, replace pre-save, pre-order, partial, clip, taster and preview-stream calls to action with verified full-release listening links, and replace preview-only players with an accessible, non-autoplay full-length media player. A service embed that remains capped to a preview when signed out does not satisfy the full-player requirement. Never expose a private master, WAV, review credential or other controlled asset to provide the player.
- Preserve the canonical live page and all unrelated working features through a merge-first candidate. Verify each listening destination resolves to the correct released recording and that the player is not duration-capped; label any unavailable platform or full-player source as unavailable rather than guessing or presenting a preview as full playback.
- Treat the transition as incomplete until signed-out desktop, tablet and mobile checks confirm that no calendar-add control or preview-only stream remains, the full-length player works without autoplay, the full listening links work, keyboard and accessible names remain correct, and there is no horizontal overflow. Then reconcile the release-folder payload, generators, manifests and reports to the verified public result so an older pre-release build cannot restore the removed calendar or preview content.

## YouTube release-day playlist and Art Track comment

- Apply this rule to every release-day update, scheduled job and automation across the project, including a later recovery of a missed release-day action. Run it only after verifying that the correct song is publicly available on YouTube under the intended artist and release identity.
- Reconcile the canonical **My Songs** playlist at `https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PL_nSZ6wLIW2VZxnB3ybJ_DmU9KgYiub3A`. Ensure the released song appears exactly once, using the best available public full-length version. Prefer an artist-owned full music video, lyric video or equivalent complete presentation over a DistroKid cover-still Art Track when it is materially better; never use a Short, excerpt, preview, duplicate, superseded mix or unrelated alternate version as the playlist representative. If the correct item is already present exactly once, leave it unchanged. Stage any replacement that requires removing an existing playlist item and follow the applicable action-time approval and verification requirements before committing it.
- Locate the separately accessible upload visibly identified as `Provided to YouTube by DistroKid`. Before commenting, inspect the existing comments and do not add a duplicate or materially equivalent playlist-link comment. When none exists, add one concise, song-specific comment in Kieran's natural voice that includes the exact **My Songs** playlist URL and a clear invitation such as “Listen to the rest of my songs here”. Vary the wording between releases without changing the destination or making unsupported claims.
- Attempt to pin the playlist-link comment only when YouTube exposes a supported **Pin** control and the action is authorised. When the DistroKid Art Track interface does not expose a pin control, leave the verified comment unpinned; this is the standard fallback and is not a failed release-day update. If no distinct accessible DistroKid-labelled Art Track exists because YouTube resolves the release to an owned full-length upload, record that state and do not comment on a guessed or incorrect video.
- Keep the workflow idempotent. Read the full live comment value before publishing, enter it with one replacement, read it back in full, recheck the video identity and playlist URL immediately before publishing, publish once, then verify that the exact comment and link persist on the intended upload. Likewise, verify the playlist entry independently after any addition or replacement.
- Record the song, selected playlist video title and ID, any displaced or rejected candidate, DistroKid Art Track title and ID or explicit unavailable state, exact comment text, pinned or unpinned state, action time and visible post-action evidence in the release-local release-day report or ledger. Do not treat playlist inclusion or a posted comment as evidence of views, listening or engagement.

## Canonical-reference graphics and sprites for EPK animation

- Apply this rule to every EPK and DanceMoves animation design, redesign, repair, task and automation. Generating graphics and sprite assets with image generation is a normal part of the animation workflow. Follow `Z:\My Songs\Workflows\epk-canonical-sprite-assets.md`.
- Every generated asset must be derived from the campaign, using the cover artwork or other canonical campaign references as its starting point. Creative deviation and variation are welcome: sprites may introduce new poses, proportions, detail, compositions or stylistic interpretations while retaining a clear relationship to the campaign's story, mood and visual identity. Inspect and supply applicable image references, record their role/path/hash, and assess campaign coherence rather than requiring an exact copy. Preserve canonical artwork unchanged; keep proposals, approvals and explicit rejections distinct.
- Keep generated originals, prompts, output hashes, alpha/edge checks, pivots and motion bounds release-local. Use DanceMoves to own motion and lifecycle, then verify the exact integrated assets at desktop, tablet and mobile sizes, including reduced motion and maximum extents, before publication.

## EPK interaction originality and cross-page reuse


- Apply this rule whenever designing or implementing a new interaction or visual effect for an EPK, including scheduled jobs and automations.
- Inspect relevant existing EPKs for reusable ideas, code, technical patterns, accessibility safeguards, performance lessons and known failure modes. Also inspect the target release's canonical live EPK, current visual-style guidance and authoritative release assets before deciding what the new effect should do.
- Never copy or transplant an effect directly from one EPK page to another. Do not duplicate a page-specific combination of markup, styling, motion choreography, timing, imagery, input mapping or visual response and present it as a new effect; changing only names, colours, speeds or assets is insufficient.
- Reuse code only after separating neutral infrastructure from the source page's creative expression. Shared utilities may cover input normalisation, event lifecycle, bounded transforms, reduced-motion handling, keyboard and touch parity, performance controls, testing or other non-visual mechanics; the target EPK's effect layer must be newly designed.
- Treat another EPK's effect as a starting insight, then improve, extend or reinterpret its function to match the target release's documented style, themes, content hierarchy and interaction needs. Seek meaningful target-specific differences in purpose, metaphor, composition, activation, movement, depth, timing and feedback, while improving accessibility, usability or performance where possible.
- Before staging or publication, document the references consulted, the generic mechanism or lesson reused, the material changes made and why the result belongs uniquely to the target EPK. Compare it with the source effects and the target visual-style guidance. If its originality and release-specific fit cannot be explained beyond superficial changes, redesign it and do not publish it.

## EPK Unicode integrity

- Apply this rule to every EPK generation, update, redesign, repair, scheduled job and automation across the project.
- Preserve intentional non-ASCII text, punctuation, symbols, emoji and scripts as content. Establish the intended characters from the canonical live page and release sources; do not replace valid Unicode with ASCII merely to make an encoding warning disappear.
- Before action-time confirmation or publication, validate the readable source and the exact WordPress transport payload as strict UTF-8. Compare material user-visible strings and code points across body text, headings, metadata, attributes, alt and accessible names, CSS generated content, JavaScript strings, and encoded calendar or download data. Scan for decoding failures, the replacement character (`U+FFFD`), broken entities or escapes, double encoding and likely mojibake; investigate suspicious sequences against the intended source rather than deleting them automatically.
- Correct every confirmed error in the appropriate layer before publishing: use correctly declared UTF-8 for ordinary HTML and text, context-appropriate HTML character references or CSS escapes where transport requires them, and one correct round of percent-encoding for URL or data payloads. Keep a readable record of any escaped supplementary-plane characters.
- Preview the exact staged payload at desktop, tablet and mobile widths. Inspect rendered text, DOM text and attributes, accessible names and computed pseudo-element `content`, and visually check screenshots for replacement glyphs, mojibake or missing characters. Any unexplained Unicode discrepancy is a publication blocker.
- After saving, repeat the checks against the persisted signed-out public response, DOM and rendered page. Do not report success unless the expected characters survive the complete source-to-transport-to-public round trip; if they do not, stop, retain the rollback revision, stage a corrected payload with a new hash and obtain confirmation before another save.

## WordPress executable-code transport and parse gate

- Apply this rule to every WordPress plugin upload, activation, update or rollback and every EPK save that contains or depends on executable JavaScript, including scheduled jobs and automations.
- Before the committing action, validate the exact plugin package and exact WordPress-bound payload rather than only the readable source. Run PHP lint where applicable, `node --check` on every shipped JavaScript file, the relevant unit and package tests, strict UTF-8 decoding, and a transport scan for entity-encoded operators or tokens that would be invalid in executable code. WordPress-bound inline scripts must avoid transport-fragile ampersand operators such as literal `&&` unless an exact persisted-payload test proves that the destination preserves them as code; prefer equivalent ampersand-free expressions for page-local adapters.
- After the committing action, arm fresh browser exception capture before loading the signed-out public page. Treat any new syntax error, uncaught initialization error, failed required plugin asset, encoded executable token such as `&#038;&#038;`, `&#38;&#38;` or `&amp;&amp;`, cue-ready state without its expected release adapter marker, or confirmed Unicode corruption as a failed deployment.
- A shared-plugin change must pass this signed-out parse and encoding sweep on every live EPK in the canonical catalogue, not only the page used for development. Keep attended playback, cue-boundary and visual checks as separate acceptance work; a clean parser is necessary but does not by itself prove choreography or timing.
- Do not call the update complete until the exact persisted package or page payload and the independent public sweep both pass. On the first confirmed failure, stop further public changes, retain or restore the last known-good version, record the affected page and exact browser error, and stage a corrected artifact for a new action-time confirmation.

## BPM-synchronised EPK motion

- Apply this rule to every EPK generation, redesign, repair, scheduled job and automation across the project.
- Any rhythmic or repeating movement must be synchronised to the canonical release master's BPM. This includes particle loops, shimmer or sparkle cycles, repeating signal paths, cloud or atmospheric loops, and any other automatically repeating animation. Pointer-, touch-, scroll- or orientation-driven responses are not repeating loops and need not be quantised.
- Establish BPM from the canonical release master before setting animation periods. Prefer verified embedded/session metadata; otherwise analyse the canonical PCM master and record the source file hash, method, candidate tempos, selected BPM and confidence in the release folder. Treat a draft generation prompt as supporting context only, not proof of the mastered tempo.
- Calculate one beat as `60 / BPM` seconds. Set each repeating duration and any phase offset to an exact integer or musically explicit fractional beat multiple. When meter and BPM note value are verified, prefer complete bar or phrase multiples; when either is unknown, state the assumption and use beat multiples without claiming bar alignment.
- Keep a release-scoped BPM variable or equivalent documented constant and a motion-duration map so future changes preserve synchronisation. Re-run computed-style timing checks after WordPress transport minification and after publication.
- `prefers-reduced-motion` remains authoritative: BPM synchronisation never overrides the requirement to stop non-essential repeating motion for users who request reduced motion.

## DanceMoves-first EPK architecture

- Apply this rule to every new visual or interactive EPK effect, every new EPK, and every material EPK redesign, repair, scheduled job and automation across the project.
- Every EPK animation or transition must be owned and orchestrated through DanceMoves, including CSS keyframes and transitions, one-off entrances, hover/focus/touch responses, parallax, tilt, audio-reactive movement, cue effects and JavaScript frame loops. A page may contain scoped CSS that renders DanceMoves-owned variables, classes or state, but it must not start, schedule, repeat, recover or tear down motion from page-local selectors, timers, listeners or animation code alone. Static transforms with no transition or animation are not motion and may remain page-local.
- Implement each new effect mechanism as reusable, page-agnostic functionality in the project-wide DanceMoves WordPress plugin first. Do not introduce a page-local implementation merely because only one EPK currently needs the effect. Generalise input acquisition, scheduling, state, lifecycle, recovery, accessibility, performance management and teardown so another EPK can use the same primitive without copying code.
- Design tilt response and BPM-synchronised motion from the outset, and implement them through DanceMoves rather than a page-local sensor listener, beat clock, cue parser, interval scheduler, playback state machine or seek-recovery routine.
- Before implementing an EPK effect, record a DanceMoves capability audit covering, where applicable, pointer and hover input, permission-aware orientation, DanceRudiments, playback pulse, cue timeline, lyric stage, interval scheduling, adaptive quality, visibility and transport recovery, reduced motion, forced colours, diagnostics and teardown. Use every applicable supported primitive that materially serves the release-specific treatment, and record each deliberate omission with its evidence-based reason; using only the clock or loading the runtime is not a complete DanceMoves integration.
- DanceMoves owns device-orientation permission and lifecycle, the 16-ticks-per-beat clock, BPM and timing-file page properties, cue dispatch and deduplication, pause/resume handling, seeking and current-time restoration, interval handlers, adaptive performance, reduced-motion behaviour and teardown. The EPK must not duplicate those responsibilities.
- The EPK page may customise only the release-specific appearance and creative response through scoped CSS, declarative effect configuration, selectors and narrowly defined rendering or cue callbacks. The page should attach/register those release-specific callbacks by calling the relevant DanceMoves primitive and passing its handlers; DanceMoves must attach and own the underlying input, playback, cue, accessibility, visibility, scheduling, reset and teardown handlers that invoke them. Those callbacks may map shared state to bounded page variables, classes, particles or drawing instructions, but must not recreate the shared acquisition, clock, cue, playback, recovery or lifecycle engine.
- When an existing EPK contains reusable mechanics, extract and validate the general primitive in DanceMoves before removing the page-local code. Preserve behavioural parity through seek, pause, resume, visibility changes, reduced motion, forced colours and teardown before publishing the migration.
- Every page must remain complete, readable and usable at neutral values when motion permission is denied, sensors are unavailable, reduced motion is requested, audio has not started or the plugin is temporarily unavailable.
- Register each supported page explicitly in DanceMoves and provide a page harness manifest plus `property-inventory.json`. Validate the exact staged payload at 1440 px, 900 px and 390 px, and require physical-device evidence before claiming sensor performance acceptance.
- Reuse the shared BPM, LRC/CUE, cue-reset and interval APIs. Do not create a parallel beat clock, duplicate `deviceorientation` listener, fixed-duration rhythmic animation or separate timing-file parser inside an EPK.
- For DanceMoves 3.x, edit canonical `assets/`, `src/` and `types/` sources and their tests, then rebuild. Never patch generated `lib/` or `.build/wordpress/` output. Deploy only the dedicated `DanceMoves-wordpress-VERSION.zip`; the browser ZIP, npm tarball, repository source ZIP and old raw-script archives are not WordPress upgrade packages. Preserve the historical WordPress folder/entrypoint and use existing handles as compatibility aliases rather than separate asset URLs.
- Before replacing the shared plugin, compare current public EPK feature markers with the candidate as well as comparing published versions. If an uncommitted feature is already live, port it into the next unused 3.x version and retain the old package as rollback; do not silently discard it or overwrite a published tag. For three-line lyrics use `DanceMovesEffects.lyricStage()` so the plugin owns neighbour selection, playback/seek lifecycle, accessibility preferences and teardown while the page owns only scoped appearance callbacks.

## Potential problems

### Elevated Windows sandbox setup can fail before commands start

- **Symptom (19 September 2026):** local commands fail before execution with `windows sandbox failed: helper_unknown_error: setup refresh had errors`.
- **Cause:** not established. A search for the exact Codex/Windows error found no credible matching external report on 19 September 2026.
- **Corrective action:** after Kieran explicitly selected the lower-overhead permanent option, set `[windows] sandbox = unelevated` in `C:\Users\Kieran\.codex\config.toml`, then restart Codex before relying on it.
- **Verification:** the saved configuration reads back exactly as `sandbox = unelevated`. The current task still requires the already-active elevated path, so a fresh task after restart must run a harmless command to confirm the setting took effect.
- **Limit:** unelevated mode deliberately provides weaker process isolation. It does not relax filesystem, network, approval, privacy or publication rules.

### Browser-native website upload capability was overlooked

- **Symptom (12 September 2026):** WordPress timing-file upload appeared blocked when looking for a locator `setInputFiles` method, even though the site exposed a Select Files button.
- **Cause supported by tool documentation:** this controlled browser offers uploads through a `filechooser` event and the chooser's `setFiles()` method, not through that locator method. The browser's `file-uploads` documentation was checked on 12 September 2026.
- **Corrective action that succeeded:** arm `tab.playwright.waitForEvent('filechooser', {timeoutMs:10000})`, click Select Files, await the chooser, check `isMultiple()`, and call `setFiles()` with exact absolute local file paths.
- **Verification:** the two Chips and Ketchup LRC files appeared as completed 12 September 2026 WordPress Media Library attachments with distinct filenames and public attachment URLs; each could be selected in its intended EPK timing field. This verifies upload and draft attachment, not a saved page setting.
- **Limit:** apply only to authorised uploads to a verified destination; confirm file type, privacy, rights and exact target first. Re-read the live field and verify the site's persisted result after any page Save. Do not use native PC control as a workaround for a website.

### PowerShell CSV property expansion fails because the requested header does not exist

- **Symptom:** `Select-Object -ExpandProperty <name>` emits `Property "<name>
  cannot be found` once per imported row after `Import-Csv` succeeds.
- **Cause when verified:** the requested property name did not match any literal
  CSV heading. Microsoft documents that `Import-Csv` creates properties from
  the header row and `Select-Object -ExpandProperty` requires the selected
  property to exist. Source checked 12 September 2026:
  <https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/microsoft.powershell.utility/import-csv>.
- **Corrective action that succeeded:** inspect one imported object's
  `PSObject.Properties.Name`, then use the exact discovered header. For the
  Fighting Entropy venue ledger this is `outlet_or_route`, not `venue` or
  `name`.
- **Verification:** the corrected expansion returned one outlet name per row
  without `ExpandPropertyNotFound` errors.
- **Limit:** do not assume a standard column name across ledgers. Inspect the
  actual header first, and do not rewrite a valid CSV for an incorrect query.

### Literal mojibake markers can break an inline Python validation command

- **Symptom:** an inline `python -c` strict-UTF-8 check exits with `SyntaxError: unterminated string literal` when suspicious Unicode or mojibake examples are embedded directly in the shell command.
- **Cause when verified:** PowerShell's command/display path transformed or truncated one of the non-ASCII marker literals before Python parsed the command.
- **Corrective action that succeeded:** keep the inline validator ASCII-only and construct required Unicode markers by code point inside Python, for example `chr(0xfffd)`, rather than placing the glyph itself in the command string. Preserve the source files unchanged.
- **Verification:** require every selected file to decode with `encoding='utf-8', errors='strict'` and explicitly assert that the constructed marker is absent; then run independent CSV integrity checks.
- **Limit:** this fixes the validator transport only. It does not prove that all possible mojibake sequences are absent or authorise rewriting intentional non-ASCII content.

### PowerShell can block a reviewed local EPK transport builder

- **Symptom:** invoking a workspace-owned `.ps1` transport builder with `& <script-path>` fails with `PSSecurityException` and reports that running scripts is disabled; the expected transport output is not created.
- **Cause when verified:** the active Windows PowerShell execution policy refused direct script execution even though the script was local and already reviewed.
- **Corrective actions that succeeded:** run the same explicit script path in a separate process with `powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File <script-path>`. Do not broaden or persist the machine or user execution policy.
- **Verification:** require the builder's expected output path, then independently hash the generated transport and run its UTF-8, placeholder, CSS-line and lyric assertions.
- **Limit:** use this only for a known, inspected workspace-owned script. It does not authorise bypassing policy for downloaded, unreviewed or unexpected scripts.

### Responsive EPK images can retain oversized HTML dimensions

- **Symptom:** a cover or artwork image becomes thousands of pixels tall in a responsive EPK even though its CSS width and aspect ratio appear correct; later sections are pushed far down the page and a full-page screenshot may look duplicated or malformed.
- **Cause when verified:** an intrinsic `height` attribute such as `height="3000"` remained a definite presentational height because the release-scoped image rule changed `width` and `aspect-ratio` but did not explicitly reset `height`.
- **Corrective actions that succeeded:** retain truthful `width` and `height` attributes for intrinsic aspect-ratio metadata, and add `height:auto` to the release-scoped responsive image rule. Re-test the exact page at fixed desktop, tablet and mobile viewports.
- **Verification:** require the computed image box to remain square at the intended responsive width, the document height to return to the expected content length, later sections to appear once in increasing document order, and horizontal overflow to be absent.
- **Limit:** apply this when a replaced CSS width coexists with fixed HTML dimensions. Do not remove intrinsic image dimensions solely to fix layout; preserve them and reset the rendered height in scoped CSS.

### Final videos can begin with a blank or black frame even when later QA frames look correct

- **Symptom:** a generated video, Short or Reel displays a blank, black or information-free starting frame, or a platform presents an unsuitable preview when no separate thumbnail has been selected, despite later sampled frames looking correct.
- **Cause when known:** the existing project checks did not consistently require inspection of the first decoded frame from the exact final encoded file. The specific causes of historical affected Reels have not been verified and must not be inferred from appearance alone.
- **Corrective actions:** design a complete and informative frame-zero composition, remove blank lead-in frames and delayed essential overlays, hold the opening composition through a short preview-safe interval, encode the final deliverable, then extract and inspect its first decoded frame. Select and verify a separate platform thumbnail or cover frame when supported, without treating that as a substitute for correcting the file.
- **Verification:** require the exact final deliverable's first decoded frame to contain the intended imagery and key truthful thumbnail information, remain legible at representative phone-thumbnail size, and contain no unintended blank, black, unrendered or transitional areas. Recheck after every rerender; for publishing work, separately verify the platform's persisted thumbnail or cover selection.
- **Limit:** this rule governs new or rerendered assets and future publishing checks. It does not prove why an existing post has a black preview, guarantee that a platform will honour a chosen thumbnail, authorise changes to already published posts, or require the first frame and selected thumbnail to be identical.

### Display encoding and protected skill paths can make a literal skill patch fail

- **Symptom:** tool output may show valid punctuation as mojibake such as `-1â€¦+1`, a literal patch using that displayed sequence fails to match, or a direct edit under `.agents\skills` is refused because the path is protected as containing a reparse point.
- **Cause when verified:** strict UTF-8 decoding of the inspected motion-skill file showed the intended `U+2026` ellipsis, so the apparent mojibake came from the output display path rather than the stored file. The safe editor separately classified the installed `.agents` path as protected; its underlying reparse target was not established.
- **Corrective actions that succeeded:** inspect suspect file bytes with a strict UTF-8 decoder and verify exact code points before editing; preserve already-correct source text; do not bypass the safe-editor restriction; place the durable rule in the writable project instructions and an available workspace-owned skill source instead.
- **Verification:** require strict UTF-8 decoding and expected code points for the suspect text, then confirm the new rule in each writable authoritative file. Do not claim that a protected installed copy was changed when it was not.
- **Limit:** this procedure distinguishes display artefacts from stored-text defects and respects protected paths; it does not prove public WordPress rendering or authorise edits through a reparse point.

### Skill validation can fail when the default Python lacks PyYAML

- **Symptom:** `quick_validate.py` exits with `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'yaml'`; a sandboxed `uv run --with pyyaml` retry may report access denied while initialising the user-level uv cache; or the uv Python process may exit from `Path.read_text()` with a CP1252 `UnicodeDecodeError` before validation begins.
- **Cause when verified:** the default Python environment did not include PyYAML, the sandbox could not write to `C:\Users\Kieran\AppData\Local\uv\cache`, and a Windows uv Python 3.13 process used the system CP1252 default for an intentional UTF-8 skill file.
- **Corrective action that succeeded:** run the official validator as `uv run --with pyyaml python -X utf8 <quick_validate.py> <skill-folder>` with approved access to uv's dependency cache. Preserve valid UTF-8 source; do not remove intentional Unicode to satisfy a locale-dependent reader.
- **Verification:** require the validator to print `Skill is valid!` for every changed skill copy.
- **Limit:** apply this only to the validator dependency/cache failure; it does not waive content review or validation failures reported after the validator starts.

### Oversized composited EPK backgrounds can crash a preview tab

- **Symptom:** the page initially renders, then the controlled browser target reports that it navigated or closed and the tab becomes `This page crashed`, often during viewport emulation or screenshots.
- **Cause when verified:** a new full-page particle plane combined repeated gradients, blending and transform/opacity compositing across the document's full height, creating an oversized GPU layer.
- **Corrective actions that succeeded:** remove the redundant plane and animate the existing particle texture. If a new decorative plane is genuinely necessary, constrain it to the viewport and avoid unnecessary blend modes or `will-change` declarations.
- **Verification:** confirm the redundant selector/markup count is zero, sample computed motion for at least one complete cycle, capture desktop/tablet/mobile screenshots and confirm the tab remains responsive.
- **Limit:** do not assume every browser crash has this cause; apply only when the same full-page compositing conditions are present.

### Cuneiform generated content can become mojibake in a local EPK preview

- **Symptom:** pseudo-element cuneiform appears as Latin-1 byte fragments even though the source contains the intended signs.
- **Cause when verified:** the local static response did not consistently declare UTF-8 for the WordPress transport fragment.
- **Corrective action that succeeded:** encode supplementary-plane characters with ASCII CSS escapes such as `\1202D` in generated `content` declarations, while preserving the intended UTF-8 glyphs and meaning map in a separate release record.
- **Verification:** inspect computed `content` and screenshots at desktop, tablet and mobile widths; confirm actual signs render rather than replacement boxes or mojibake.
- **Limit:** apply this to generated CSS content in transport fragments; ordinary page text should instead be served with the correct charset.

### Low server disk space can masquerade as WordPress JSON or upload corruption

- **Symptom:** an otherwise valid block-editor save reports `Updating failed. The response is not a valid JSON response`, a larger authenticated request returns an nginx HTML 500 while a smaller request succeeds, or a validated plugin/media upload reports `Failed to write file to disk`.
- **Cause when verified:** Kieran confirmed low server disk space during the page-252 incident. The editor error was HTML replacing the expected WordPress JSON response, and the upload error was PHP failing to write its temporary file. WordPress Site Health still called the content directories writable, so permissions-only checks did not detect the capacity fault.
- **Corrective actions that succeeded:** stop after one failed mutation; verify the persisted editor source and public markers remain on the preceding revision; confirm no partial plugin row exists; reload the clean persisted editor state; cancel further saves/uploads until server capacity is remediated. Preserve exact payload and package hashes for a later confirmed retry.
- **Detection and verification:** before every WordPress EPK write, read free bytes/quota use from a host-level source when available and record its source/time. Warn below 1 GiB or 10% free; block non-essential writes below 256 MiB, at 95% quota use, on any host exhaustion warning, or when a temporary-file probe fails. After space is freed, record the improved capacity, perform one small reversible write/upload with action-time approval, remove the probe, then perform the exact intended write once and verify the signed-out page.
- **Limit:** WordPress REST reads, Site Health directory writability and high PHP POST/memory limits do not prove adequate free space. A healthy aggregate reading also does not exclude a full temp mount, inode exhaustion, quota mismatch or permissions; use host logs if the signature persists.

### Very tall animated EPKs can produce duplicate-looking full-page screenshots

- **Symptom:** a full-page capture appears to repeat complete players, story sections, artwork or lyrics despite prior feature-parity checks.
- **Cause when verified:** the published page-252 DOM had one instance of every canonical section with sequential, non-overlapping document-space rectangles, while clean fixed-viewport captures showed no duplication. The roughly 8,500-pixel animated document produced a browser stitching/compositor artefact.
- **Corrective actions that succeeded:** check DOM counts and section rectangles before changing the page; capture fixed viewports at 1440, 900 and 390 pixels; treat animated full-page captures as supplementary evidence only.
- **Verification:** require one DOM instance per section, increasing non-overlapping positions, zero positive horizontal overflow, clean fixed-viewport captures and a signed-out HTML count for the player and chapter controls.
- **Limit:** apply this only when those structural checks pass; otherwise investigate real duplicated markup or overlapping layout.

### WordPress `wpautop` can insert invisible grid or flex children

- **Symptom:** a row that fits in the local source wraps after WordPress rendering, such as chapter buttons appearing 3+2 or artwork text moving below its image; unexpected gaps may appear in button or footer rows.
- **Cause when verified:** the public page-252 DOM contained alternating direct-child `<br>` elements between chapter buttons and empty direct-child `<p>` elements in artwork, action and cosmology containers. Grid and flex layout treated them as real items even though they had no visible content.
- **Corrective actions that succeeded:** inspect the public DOM's direct children with display, dimensions and positions; hide only each proven formatting artefact with release-scoped direct-child selectors such as `.release-root .chapter-grid > br` and `.release-root .artwork-grid > p:empty`; keep all non-empty paragraphs and nested/intentional line breaks.
- **Verification:** apply the candidate CSS to exact saved WordPress-rendered HTML; require five chapter buttons on one row at desktop/tablet, the intended artwork image/text row, deliberate mobile stacking, zero overflow and clean targeted screenshots at 1440, 900 and 390 pixels.
- **Limit:** never apply page-wide `br` or empty-paragraph suppression. The workaround must remain release-scoped and evidence-led.

# Project-local models for every DanceFlow project

Standing instruction from Kieran, 16 October 2026: install all downloaded model weights and model-specific caches in a `models/` directory within the owning project, and add `models/` to that project's `.gitignore`.

- Apply to every DanceFlow component, new project, integration, chat, agent and recurring workflow. Resolve the actual owning project before installing; do not default downloads to C:, a user profile, an unrelated project or a global model cache.
- Route framework download/cache settings, adapter model paths and trained-data locations beneath that project-local models root. Keep model identity, revision, licence and file-hash manifests there where supported. Weights, caches and private model evidence stay out of Git, wheels, source archives and public release assets.
- Download only models useful for the authorised task and permitted by existing licence/cost/privacy/download requirements. The storage rule does not require a catalogue-wide download or another model run.
- Before migrating existing weights, inventory current references and consumers, preserve originals and manifests, stage the new location, verify actual loading and update references. Do not silently move/delete existing models or claim every running chat has adopted a saved rule.

## Preflight temporary-directory access

Before any long, costly, model-backed, media-rendering, build, test, packaging or release command that creates private or atomic temporary directories, run a disposable preflight with the exact runtime, user or sandbox, and intended output parent. The probe must create a unique directory, write and read a file, atomically rename it, then remove the file and directory.

Keep temporary data and caches under the owning project on `Z:`; model weights and model-specific caches remain under that project's ignored `models/`. If the probe returns `WinError 5`, access denied, a rename failure or a cleanup failure, stop before the expensive command. Preserve the exact error and use the documented narrowly approved unrestricted recovery from the outset only when the task requires it. Use a fresh output directory and do not repeat the already-failing full job in the restricted shell.

After any unrestricted recovery, verify that the final output is readable and writable from the normal managed environment. If it is not, copy the completed bytes into a pre-created project-local directory that passed the probe, verify hashes, and retain the original failure and recovery evidence. Do not edit global ACLs, switch caches to `C:`, terminate unrelated processes or claim the underlying permission problem is fixed. Repositories that own temporary-directory creation should provide and test a reusable preflight helper rather than relying only on this instruction.
